\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ move puts  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  focus on U.S. asset marts-dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 1999\par 13:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 14 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan's moves to more flexibly implement its easy money policy have shifted the impetus for the dollar's downtrend away from Japan and squarely on to jittery U.S. asset markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ on Wednesday said it would expand its range of market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , upholding a promise last month to Japan's Group of Seven (G7) partners and paving the way for possible concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, they said. \par \par \uc2  They added that the possibility of joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was expected to increase if U.S. asset markets extend their recent downward slide. \par \par \uc2  "Just like Japan, the United States and other nations surely want to avoid an excessive fall in the dollar against the yen to avoid disruption in global financial markets and a crash on Wall Street," said Michihisa Tanimoto, deputy general manager of securities investment section at Sumitomo Life Insurance. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was not expecting the dollar to fall significantly against the yen, as expectations of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were likely to limit aggressive speculative sales below the dollar's 1999 low of 103.20/25 yen. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  put its words into action on Thursday morning by supplying additional yen to the Tokyo money market to ensure liquidity. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the dollar spiked about 1-yen to 107.98 yen after the BOJ's announcement, although by early New York trading hours it had reversed course and drifted lower to about 106 yen, mainly due to declines in U.S. bond and share prices. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury prices fell for a fourth consecutive session on Wednesday, with the benchmark 30-year bond dropping 21/32 to 97-31/32 and the yield rising to 6.28 percent. The Dow Jones industrial average slumped 184.90 points to close at 10,232.16 \par \par \uc2  As of 0900 GMT, the dollar was quoted around 106.80 yen, compared with 106.50/52 yen in New York late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  CLOSER ATTENTION TO U.S. ASSET MARKETS, EURO \par \par \uc2  In the near term, the market is expected to keep an eye open for details of Japan's planned economic stimulus package, due to be announced next month, but there is much more interest in the fate of U.S. asset markets and economic fundamentals in the euro zone, market sources said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the dollar/yen rate would be driven greatly by factors in Japan after the highly anticipated decision by the BOJ was announced," said Hitoshi Imamura, deputy manager at Nippon Credit Bank. \par \par \uc2  Imamura said further selling pressure in U.S. asset markets may trigger a renewed downtrend in the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar/yen rate seems to be out of the market's focus. The market is rather concerned about how the dollar itself moves. Also the focus is on the euro given the growing optimism over the outlook for its (the euro zone's) economy." \par \par \uc2  A senior dealer at a European bank said the yen was not likely to strengthen beyond 105 yen to the dollar in the near term. \par \par \uc2  "We are not likely to see the kind of overall yen strength that we've witnessed over the past several months," the dealer said. "The dollar/yen rate is expected to be rangebound, probably locked in a range from 105 to 108 yen per dollar in the near term, while the focus will be on economic fundamentals in Europe and movements on Wall Street." ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473-3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dvae02123\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
