\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW:Ex-BOJ Nakagawa -2:Money Mkt Surplus Ok \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 1999\par 07:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan is unlikely to ease monetary policy when its policy board meets next week, Yukitsugu Nakagawa, deputy chairman of the Institute for International Policy Studies, said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A former executive director of the BOJ, Nakagawa told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview, "I don't think the BOJ will ease monetary policy at its policy board meeting next Wednesday." He added, "It would be pointless for the BOJ to take further monetary easing steps." \par \par \uc2  Nakagawa said that any additional monetary easing measures taken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would neither jump-start the nation's economy nor weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  "A monetary easing by the BOJ such as quantitative easing would not further stimulate the economy and an unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ would not help to weaken the yen, as the nation's money market is already overflowing with surplus funds," he said. \par \par \uc2  An unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurs when the BOJ leaves the yen funds sold for the dollars bought during \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money market. \par \par \uc2  Nakagawa noted that the BOJ's daily money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave Y1 trillion in excess funds above the requirement is ample enough for Japanese banks and companies. \par \par \uc2  "The Y1 trillion in daily excess funds goes to the call brokers, where most of it usually remains. Banks are not that eager to lend and companies are not that keen to borrow, as they are busy with restructuring or cutting back capital investments," he said. \par \par \uc2  Turning to the recent rise of the yen against the dollar, Nakagawa expressed some concerns that a sharp yen rise could hurt Japanese corporations, thereby slowing down Japan's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "A sharp yen rise against the dollar is not good, as Japanese corporates would not be able to cope with this, thus affecting Japan's economy adversely," he said. \par \par \uc2  However, he also pointed out that a strengthening of the yen is not bad for Japan as long as the trend is gradual. \par \par \uc2  "A strong yen is not necessarily bad for Japan as long as this strengthening is gradual. It is also a reflection of Japan's value as a country to the overseas markets," he said, adding "The current dollar-yen levels are not a problem for Japan." \par \par \uc2  At 0500 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y107.90, and the euro was trading at Y115.52. \par \par \uc2  In the future, Nakagawa said, "I think the dollar trading at Y100-Y110 is appropriate. An excessively strong or weak yen is not desirable." \par \par \uc2  If any sharp moves occur in the yen against the dollar or other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nakagawa said that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to "smooth out" these moves. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ should be a smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes big (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) moves into small ones," he said. \par \par \uc2  Nakagawa added that the BOJ and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  have differing views on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though he said that such differences are "natural." \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ thinks that a strong yen reflects the strength of Japan as a country to the overseas markets, while the MOF believes that it is bad for the nation's economy. But, such differences in opinions are natural," he said, "What's important is that the overall government policies are coherent." \par \par \uc2  Referring to recent media reports of the government's large second supplementary budget, Nakagawa said, "The size of the extra second budget is likely to be Y13 trillion or more. (Finance Minister Kiichi) Miyazawa has suggested Y5 trillion-Y6 trillion in direct fiscal injection is needed." \par \par \uc2  Nakagawa said that more economic stimulus is needed as Japan's economy recovery is not self-sustaining. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's economy has bottomed, but its recovery is not autonomous," he said, citing forecasts for weak gross domestic product growth this fiscal year and next fiscal year. "I see GDP growth at 0.3% in fiscal 1999 and in fiscal 2000. This is also based on forecasts given by Nomura Research Institute." \par \par \uc2  Although more issuance of Japanese government bonds is planned for this fiscal year and next fiscal year, Nakagawa did not express much concern, saying "Any rise in long-term interest rates due to extra supply is natural." \par \par \uc2  He also said that the BOJ would likely oppose any moves by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the government to purchase more JGBs. "The BOJ will likely oppose any calls for it to purchase additional JGBs," he said. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 07-10-99 \par \par \uc2  0509GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dva707bdj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
