\par\par \b \uc2  Robert Rubin tells investors they must bear risks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 1999\par 05:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Oct 4 (Reuters) - Former Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin returned to the world's financial capital on Monday to remind investors risk is still the flipside of reward, warning they must be accountable for their choices. \par \par \uc2  A year after Rubin and Washington colleagues were credited with preventing a global market meltdown, the former Wall Street trader said a sense of complacency has grown each time another potential catastrophe was successfully avoided. \par \par \uc2  "Just as they reap the benefits of their decisions, private creditors and investors must also bear the risk of failure," Rubin told members of the Bond Market Association at a gala dinner. \par \par \uc2  "Financial markets have fluctuated between extremes and this should be a sobering warning," he added. \par \par \uc2  Speaking days after Ecuador defaulted on its debt, becoming the first nation to renege on a Brady bond, Rubin said his comments were intended in general terms, adding it is now up to someone else to worry about issues like these. \par \par \uc2  HINTS OF RETURN TO FINANCIAL WORLD \par \par \uc2  Welcomed with a standing ovation as he collected an award for his service, Rubin said he was happy to be a private citizen living in New York. He also teased the room full of bankers with hints he may soon return to the business he left six years ago. \par \par \uc2  "I expect to rejoin (the financial world) rather shortly," Rubin said, before assuring his audience "this was not meant to be a job wanted ad." \par \par \uc2  Rubin left the administration in July and has offered few specifics about what may be ahead, saying he wants to enjoy a little time off. \par \par \uc2  Proving he still speaks the market's language however, Rubin pointed to a major benefit of private life. "While I would argue my points in Washington, in the private sector I could just go short," he said to laughter. \par \par \uc2  PRAISE FOR SUMMERS ON POLICY STATEMENTS \par \par \uc2  Rubin praised his successor Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, saying he is doing everything right in articulating the administration's policy on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I think Secretary (Lawrence) Summers has done an outstanding job communicating the U.S. policy," Rubin said when asked whether the U.S. may need to alter the way in which it has articulated its policy on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  To date, Summers has often said a strong dollar remains in the best interest of the United States, a phrase closely associated with Rubin's tenure at the Treasury. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market dealers have received a slightly mixed message, especially as the dollar has slumped some 15 percent against the Japanese yen since Summers moved from the number two spot to the Secretary's office in July. \par \par \uc2  Analysts attribute yen gains to Japan's improved economic outlook. Asked to speak directly about Japan, Rubin, fresh from a trip to Asia said "a lot has been accomplished" - acknowledging, however, that much lies ahead. \par \par \uc2  Since a recent meeting of financial officials from the Group of Seven rich nations, the dollar has recovered some ground against the yen as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market traders speculated Tokyo may have succeeded in getting its partner nations' to offer it help in taming the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  But since the September 25 meeting, no \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market openly. \par \par \uc2  In Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Economic Planning Minister Taichi Sakaiya said he sees no need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar stabilises around 105 to 106 yen. \par \par \uc2  ((-NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dva502jaa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
