\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - Dollar chatters in tight ranges, eyes on stocks, FOMC. \b0\par\par\uc2 745 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 1999\par 19:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In New York story headlined "Dollar chatters in tight ranges..." please read in the second paragraph "While the Fed is expected to leave its key fed funds rate unchanged at 5.25 percent," instead of " ...5.50 percent." Corrects fed funds rate. A corrected version follows: \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Oct 5 (Reuters) - The dollar idled nervously in slim ranges at midday on Tuesday as investors awaited the Federal Reserve's decision on U.S. interest rates and cautiously eyed U.S. asset markets. \par \par \uc2  While the Fed is expected to leave its key fed funds rate unchanged at 5.25 percent, the sense of mystery surrounding the Fed's meeting, as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings in Frankfurt and London, kept the market on eggshells, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Although there's a wide consensus the Fed will do nothing, nobody really wants to do anything ahead of the announcement. So, I think we'll sit tight," said Kathy Jones, director of futures research at Prudential Securities Inc. \par \par \uc2  At 1200 EDT/1600 GMT, the dollar was barely changed against the Japanese yen at 106.92/02 from 106.90/98 after a brief run to session highs above 107 yen as Japanese officials spoke out about the yen's rapid rise. It had firmed from 106.23/31 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also hardly budged in New York morning trading, slipping to $1.0690/97 from $1.0693/99 as the U.S. session kicked in, after losing nearly half a cent overnight from $1.0737/41 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had gotten a lift early in the New York session from a brighter tone in the U.S. stock market, which was polished by optimism the Fed would leave rates steady for now. \par \par \uc2  Prospects of a hike in interest rates as a preemptive strike against inflation, normally seen as positive for U.S. assets, have recently rattled U.S. stocks and bonds. \par \par \uc2  But for now, the dollar largely shrugged off a push higher by the Dow Jones industrial average, which stood 56 points higher at 10,456 near noon. Traders said the dollar would likely trade in lock-step with the Dow after the Fed decision. \par \par \uc2  A positive reaction from the Dow could lift the dollar through resistance near its session highs above 107 yen and even help it push toward a key technical level near 108.30, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Market players mulled the future of the Japanese economy after Economic Planning Agency Minister Taichi Sakaiya said it was possible for the government to revise upward its current forecast of 0.5 percent growth for the current fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  Sakaiya also added to speculation about a new fiscal stimulus package, saying the details of key points could be hammered out by the end of October. \par \par \uc2  Analysts see an extra budget as a potential key to unlocking international assistance in taming the yen's rise, which Japanese officials fear will damage Japan's fledgling recovery. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, the dollar got a slight boost after Sakaiya said he saw no need for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar stabilizes around 105/106 yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi said he welcomed the recent stability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but warned a quick-paced yen rise remains undesirable, adding it must be dealt with appropriately. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said news that Germany's unemployment lines lengthened again helped offset stronger French consumer confidence data and may keep the euro under some pressure as a near-term interest rate hike appears unlikely. \par \par \uc2  Germany's Federal Labor Office said there were 9,000 more unemployed in September, seasonally adjusted, marking the seventh straight rise. On an unadjusted basis, the total number of jobless fell to 3.943 million from 4.024 million in August. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar was also stagnant against the Swiss franc, rising to 1.4885/95 francs from 1.4880/88 francs in early U.S. dealing and 1.4834/42 francs late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The British pound slipped further to $1.6523/33 from $1.6547/55 late Monday as traders eyed the Bank of England's two-day policy meeting which begins on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Economists are now expecting another rate rise from the current 5.25 percent before the end of the year, but say that move may occur in November, not this week. \par \par \uc2  ((Elizabeth Lazarowitz, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1607)) (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dva502iao\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
