\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar up at U.S. start as market eyes rate moves. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 1999\par 16:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Oct 4 (Reuters) - The dollar hopscotched to small gains at the start of trading on Monday and will likely take its cues from U.S. asset markets as participants await more news on where global interest rates may move next. \par \par \uc2  Central bankers will meet in Washington, London and Frankfurt this week and while many investors think official rate hikes may be premature, traders said the trend of rising rates is likely to hurt the dollar and help the euro. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is probably going to lag developments in the bond and stock markets, rising if they should have a good day and coming off if they post losses," Bank Julius Baer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist David Durrant said. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, the euro rallied to a two month high against the dollar as traders turned last week's pointed comments from three European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials into speculation the Bank may be ready to lift its 2.50 percent refinancing rate. \par \par \uc2  But nervous traders, still not entirely convinced that the time is right for tighter credit conditions in the 11-nation euro bloc, quickly took profits, allowing the greenback to start New York trading at 1.0707/11 per euro. It closed at 1.0719/24 per euro on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's President, Chief Economist and Vice President last week warned about the dangers of rising prices pressures and said the bank would prefer to act in time to curb such developments. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose more forcefully against the Japanese yen, starting the day at 105.98/06 yen compared with 105.12/22 yen at Friday's close after a key confidence indicator failed to deliver the fireworks some traders had expected last week. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's "tankan" survey of corporate sentiment, conducted in September, showed the big manufacturers' index rose to minus 22, compared with economists' average forecast of minus 26 and the June survey's minus 37. It was the strongest improvement in 12 years. \par \par \uc2  While Tokyo welcomed the news, government spokesman Hiromu Nonaka warned the assessment of the economy remains severe. \par \par \uc2  The prospect of global action aimed at restraining yen strength seems to have faded further as dealers felt Tokyo probably has not yet done enough to help itself to secure agreements for a possible bout of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did leave a 1.5 trillion yen surplus in the banking system, more money than usual but less than the 1.9 trillion left in last week, but dealers were not convinced this move translated into easier monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  In turn, "Further dollar gains against the yen are likely, a move above the 106.35 area would encourage a test of last week's 107.25 high," said Mellon Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Marc Chandler. \par \par \uc2  Dealers agreed the dollar's next move will depend largely on what the Federal Reserve decides at Tuesday's meeting and only one of 30 primary dealers of U.S. government securities, polled by Reuters last week, bet on a rate hike. \par \par \uc2  The Fed lifted rates twice this summer but has not fully taken back last year's quick-paced easings aimed a preventing a major market meltdown after Russia declared a debt moratorium and effectively devalued the rouble. \par \par \uc2  "In the longer run a rate hike would probably be good for the dollar, but in the short term there may be some downside and possible nervousness," said INGBarings senior vice president John McCarthy. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the British pound started U.S. trading nearly flat at $1.6552/60 but quickly extended gains as activity got underway amid more talk the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee may raise rates at its two-day meeting which starts on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar however inched higher against the Swiss franc to 1.4860/68 from 1.4838/48 while it slipped against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4725/35 from C$1.4739/49. The Australian dollar rose to $0.6592/99 from $0.6554/64 as gold prices continued to push higher. \par \par \uc2  ((-North American Treasury Desk (212) 859-1639)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dva402pfp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
