\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Japan output surges, but strong yen a worry. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 1999\par 07:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 29 (Reuters) - Japan's industrial production posted its biggest rise in nearly three years in August, but the stubbornly strong yen clouds prospects for a sustained recovery, the government said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Output at Japan's factories and mines jumped 4.6 percent from the previous month, the biggest climb since January 1997, boosted by vehicle production and some seasonal factors, the Ministry of International Trade and Industry (MITI) said in its latest monthly report. \par \par \uc2  MITI UPGRADES ASSESSMENT \par \par \uc2  This was above the average forecast of 3.7 percent growth in a Reuters survey of economists this week and at the high end of the 11 forecasts. \par \par \uc2  But the ministry lowered its forecast for this month's manufacturing output to a 1.3 drop from an original 0.2 percent rise and predicted a further 0.7 percent dip in October. \par \par \uc2  "Production and shipments appear to be improving but the prospects for continued recovery in final demand are uncertain and will continue to require a close watch," the ministry said in its report. \par \par \uc2  This was an improvement from the previous monthly report, in which the ministry had said only that production and shipments were hitting bottom. \par \par \uc2  But an official expressed concern that after a time lag of several months, the high yen will hurt Japan's exports and that another worry was the possibility that support from government policy measures would fade. \par \par \uc2  The data also did not reflect last week's earthquake in Taiwan, a major competitor for Japan's semiconductor producers. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets shrugged off Wednesday's output data. \par \par \uc2  Separately, the Finance Ministry's regional finance bureaux said economic conditions nationwide remained severe but were improving somewhat thanks to government policy measures. \par \par \uc2  The report said that for the Kanto region, centred on Tokyo, it would take more time for private demand to recover fully as worries remain over severe income and job conditions and the effects of a recent rapid rise in the yen on corporate profits. \par \par \uc2  YEN'S RISE ALARMS GOVERNMENT \par \par \uc2  The yen is up some 16 percent from its year lows hit in May and, at around 106 yen to the dollar on Wednesday, is stronger than the August average of around 113 yen, according to Reuters Graphics data. \par \par \uc2  The yen has surged since the Japanese economy began showing signs of growth in early June, as foreign investors have poured money into yen assets in the belief that the world's second biggest economy was finally pulling out of its worst postwar recession. \par \par \uc2  But the yen's rise has alarmed the government, which fears it will thwart the nascent recovery by eroding the yen value of Japanese companies' overseas earnings and inflaming deflationary pressures on the economy. \par \par \uc2  MUM ON G7 JOINT \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 , LOOSER BOJ POLICY \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  promised at a weekend meeting of the Group of Seven industrial powers that it would continue policies to support the economy until a recovery led by private demand was in place in return for a G7 statement of shared "concern" over the yen. \par \par \uc2  On the fiscal side, the government on Wednesday approved plans to spend an already-budgeted 500 billion yen on high-speed trains, airports, telecoms infrastructure ands other products. \par \par \uc2  And Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said a new cabinet, which Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi wants to launch on Friday, will immediately start work on a supplementary budget, expected to include some 4-5 trillion yen in direct fiscal spending. \par \par \uc2  The yen swung violently in the aftermatch of the G7 meeting as markets tried to gauge whether the Bank of Japan had promised to further ease its already ultra-easy monetary policy to weaken the yen and whether the United States would join \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa refused on Wednesday to comment on the chances of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying only that the G7's vow to "cooperate as appropriate" was sufficient. \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami, still in Washington, also kept the markets guessing overnight on his monetary policy plans, reiterating that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was looking for ways to diversify its money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  but insisting its zero-interest rate policy had already resulted in "considerable" liquidity for financial markets. ((Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9t02xlo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
