\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-European cenbank VP Noyer sees strong euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 1999\par 23:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 29 (Reuters) - European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President Christian Noyer said on Wednesday he was confident the euro has room to appreciate, especially as euro bloc nations show signs of economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The euro, now stabilising from the lifetime low it hit this summer, blipped slightly higher after Noyer spoke and ended New York trading almost a full cent higher against the dollar at $1.0640. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmly based on price stability and therefore has a strong potential to appreciate, Noyer told journalists at a conference hosted by the European Institute. \par \par \uc2  Therefore I am "confident the euro is and will be a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. \par \par \uc2  Pointing to recent economic data, particularly improved business confidence data in the 11-nation euro zone area, Noyer said there was reason to expect European growth to keep accelerating. \par \par \uc2  "There have been encouraging signs," he said, noting however that further evidence needs to be amassed to be sure the recovery is secure. \par \par \uc2  Even though second quarter growth figures were slightly weaker than many analysts had predicted, Noyer said this should not jeopardise growth forecasts for the year as a whole, because activity should accelerate in the second half. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund recently forecast Europe's economy would grow 1.9 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  The prospect for stronger growth has sparked talk of rising interest rates and Noyer fanned such speculation on Monday when he said the bank may need to take preemptive action to keep inflation under its two percent goal. \par \par \uc2  By reviving talk that a tightening bias is creeping into the ECB's policy making stance, Noyer helped the euro recover ground against the yen and dollar on Monday. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday however he said he was "a little surprised by the market's reaction," noting his remarks, made to the European Parliament, were meant in a more general manner. \par \par \uc2  "We react in a forward looking fashion and it is best to avoid reacting too late," Noyer said, declining to be drawn out further on the ECB's future steps on interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  The ECB's policy-setting council meets next week for the latest review of European interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The bank cut it key refinancing rate to 2.50 percent in April and only seven out of 38 analysts polled by Reuters last week expect the Bank to change interest in 1999. \par \par \uc2  Turning back to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Noyer said the euro's near 14 percent decline against the dollar during the first seven months of its life was merely a correction from earlier levels where the euro's component \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had risen sharply. \par \par \uc2  An incorrect assessment that the Asia crisis would harm the United States more than Europe had helped the mark rise sharply against the dollar in the fall of 1998. \par \par \uc2  "So the trend was just reversed and all in all the euro is very stable," Noyer said adding the dollar's exchange rate against the euro "is not the problem". \par \par \uc2  Rather, the yen's recent sharp appreciation against the dollar and euro has worried international leaders because of its potentially negative knock-on effects for the global economy, Noyer noted. "The problem the Group of Seven wanted to address was really a problem with the yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  The group of rich nations this weekend said it shared Japan's concern about the yen's appreciation and since Monday the yen has dropped off against the the dollar and euro amid market speculation the group may have pledged to help \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Such help may be expressed in concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and traders have scrambled to cover short dollar positions since Monday, leaving the dollar near 107 yen, up over 3-1/2 yen from the 3-1/2 year lows it hit last week. \par \par \uc2  Noyer fanned talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday by saying the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stood ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary. On Wednesday he sounded a similar message, saying "such action cannot be excluded." But he refused to give any hints on the likelihood or possible timing of such a move. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9t02ral\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
