\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS-Euro makes most of BOJ policy confusion. \b0\par\par\uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 1999\par 07:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The euro was quietly firmer on the yen on Tuesday as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bowed to persistent speculation about an easing in monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The euro trailed dollar/yen higher overnight as comments out of the fringes of the G7 meeting in Washington suggested Japan had at least given its partners the impression it would undertake aggressive new stimulus measures. \par \par \uc2  That was enough to see the euro as high as 111.24 yen from 108.88 in Tokyo on Monday, but it eased from there to 110.74/90 in the afternoon, reflecting general scepticism in the local market that the Bank of Japan would take the big steps needed. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also noted that volumes were very light, with local exporters and institutions content to hug the sidelines with book closing for the end of the quarter and fiscal half year just two days away. \par \par \uc2  Nor was the euro helped by comments by Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke that the rise in euro-zone money supply growth should not be overinterpreted and he saw no grounds for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to change policy. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with Reuters he said: "In principle I would say that...one can easily continue with a steady hand (policy)." \par \par \uc2  The remarks stood in contrast to those from ECB Vice President Christian Noyer that a preemptive rise in interest rates might be needed to stop the euro area exceeding its non-inflationary growth potential. \par \par \uc2  All of which confirmed the suspicions of most analysts that official interest rates would probably not rise before year-end but more than likely in the first quarter of 2000. \par \par \uc2  Monetary policy was also a preoccupation for the yen as various Japanese and G7 officials talked up the prospect for a substantial easing in policy. \par \par \uc2  Typical was a comment from Federal Reserve Governor William McDonough that Japan had made a very clear commitment to more economic steps at the G7. \par \par \uc2  And Economic Planning Minister Taichi Sakaiya stated bluntly that the Bank of Japan should ease monetary policy further. \par \par \uc2  But dealers had doubts, noting that none of the commentators were from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself. \par \par \uc2  Instead, the BOJ had on Tuesday morning passed on another chance to be more accommodative by keeping its money market surplus unchanged at one trillion yen, so confounding speculation it would add more funds. \par \par \uc2  A source in the Japanese delegation visiting Washington told Reuters that using unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way to supply extra liquidity is unrealistic under \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current policy framework. \par \par \uc2  Unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is favoured by those calling for a quantitative easing in policy, since it would boost liquidity in the money market and, theoretically, lower the value of the yen by increasing its supply. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted that if the BOJ were still against unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it must also be opposed to all other forms of quantitative easing, such as underwriting JGBs, since the end result would be the same. \par \par \uc2  And without some sort of quantitative step, analysts seriously doubt the rest of the G7 would commit to coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or that the market would lose its appetite for yen. \par \par \uc2  The market may have to wait for the next BOJ board meeting on October 13 before finding out one way or the other. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Swiss franc was quoted at 69.15/20 from 68.01/06 yen here on Monday, while a broadly firmer pound climbed to 174.14/26 yen from 171.23/35. ((Wayne Cole, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8584 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9s03362\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
