\par\par \b \uc2  World Markets: Shares Rise In Europe, Decline In Tokyo, Hong Kong \b0\par\par\uc2 1,090 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 1999\par 06:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Shares in Europe ended higher Monday, with a rebound on Wall Street and a recovery in the dollar helping to calm investors' fears about a meltdown in U.S. markets. Latin American and Canadian shares also finished higher. Asian stock markets ended lower, with Tokyo's key index slipping for the third straight session, while Hong Kong stocks dropped more than 2%. \par \par \uc2  U.S. shares gave up earlier solid gains to finish modestly higher, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average adding 24.06 to 10303.39 after losing more than 500 points, or 4.9%, last week, its biggest one-week point loss ever. But as the major European stock markets were closing at 11:30 a.m. EDT, the Dow had been up 1%. Meanwhile, the dollar jumped nearly 1 1/2 yen in New York amid indications that Japan will work with international central bankers to stop the dollar's slide against the yen. At a weekend meeting, finance ministers from the Group of Seven major industrialized nations pledged to monitor yen exchange-rate developments and "cooperate as appropriate." \par \par \uc2  In London, the FT-SE 100 index ended up 141.0 points, or 2.4%, at 6078.6, following Friday's 0.5% decline. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Scotland's surprise 20.85 billion pound ($34.24 billion) bid for NatWest Friday turned up the heat in the banking sector, with Royal Bank of Scotland just one of the names mentioned to launch a counterbid. Royal Bank of Scotland's shares jumped 115 pence, or 9.0%, to 1,391 pence. \par \par \uc2  Retail banks climbed 5.6% as a sector. NatWest shares closed up 122 pence, or 9.0%, at 1,476 pence and Lloyds TSB rose 59 pence, or 8.1%, to 786.5 pence. Investors are trying to predict where a counter bid will come from, with HSBC Holdings, Abbey National, French bank Societe Generale or Dutch group ING Groep all names in the frame. Bank of Scotland shares closed up 45.5 pence, or 6.0%, at 795.5 pence. \par \par \uc2  Frankfurt's Xetra DAX index of 30 blue chips rose 53.11 points, or 1.0%, at 5239.64, after dropping 2.1% on Friday. \par \par \uc2  In Paris, the CAC-40 index of most actively traded French stocks ended up 55.73 points, or 1.2%, at 4596.60, after Friday's 1.3% decline. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Europe: Zurich's Swiss Market index closed up 95.7 points, or 1.4%; Madrid's IBEX-35 index rose 90.10 points, or 0.9%; Milan's MIBtel index gained 47 points, or 0.2%; Amsterdam's AEX index rose 8.07 points, or 1.5%, and Stockholm's Swedish General index ended up 37.17 points, or 1.0%. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Stoxx Index of 592 European companies rose 4.35 points, or 1.4%, to close at 309.38. The Dow Jones Euro Stoxx Index, which tracks 297 companies in countries that joined the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , closed up 3.20 points at 323.38. \par \par \uc2  In Moscow, the benchmark RTS index climbed 3.47 points, or 4.1%, to close at 87.67, following Friday's 0.9% decline. Trading volume slipped to $7.4 million from $5.0 million on Friday. \par \par \uc2  In Latin America, Argentina's Merval index jumped 19.94 points, or 3.9%, to end at 526.48 after Friday's 0.6% slide as investors bought the market up after last week's losses. \par \par \uc2  Mexico City's key IPC index rose 74.88 points, or 1.5%, to finish at 5055.76 after after Friday's 0.1% rise. Mexican stocks closely tracked the Dow industrials throughout the session. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's Bovespa index at the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange rose 9.35 points, or 0.1%, to at 11542.17, extending Friday's jump of 1.3%. \par \par \uc2  Share prices on the Bogota Stock Exchange closed sharply higher on the news that the Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had eliminated the peso's trading band and let it float freely, brokers said. The IBB general index rose 1.6% to 910.30. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced Saturday night that it would scrap the five-year-old crawling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  band, which had set a moving upper and lower limit for the peso. \par \par \uc2  In Canada, the Toronto Stock Exchange's 300 composite index rose 132.31 points, or 2%, to 6895.69, buoyed by a stunning 20.7% rise in the gold group. Mining stocks and gold prices jumped after 15 European central banks vowed over the weekend to sharply curtail the amount of gold they would sell in the next five years. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, Japan's Nikkei 225 index fell 50.67 points, or 0.3%, to close at 16821.06, extending Friday's 454.03-point drop. Losers beat winners 7 to 5, while 346.7 million shares were traded. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei has shed a total of 1111.73 points over the last three sessions on worries about the continued strengthening of the yen and sharp declines in U.S. stocks. \par \par \uc2  Japanese stock prices fell in the absence of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the G-7 meeting. Many traders had believed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would buy dollars to stem the yen's rise. Japanese officials believe that the yen has strengthened too much and could derail a budding economic recovery. A higher yen makes Japanese exports less cost-competitive in global markets. \par \par \uc2  Japan bought dollars in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets several times over the summer to curb the yen's gains, but without success. As recently as May, the dollar bought more than 124 yen, but since has fallen about 16%. \par \par \uc2  "There is disappointment that the BOJ acted as usual," said Kazunori Jinnai of Daiwa Securities SB Capital Markets. \par \par \uc2  In Hong Kong, strong selling pressure by major overseas brokerage firms also hurt sentiment, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng index fell 271.61 points, or 2.1%, to close at 12760.46, hitting a six-week low. Friday, the blue-chip indicator dropped 182.37 points, or 1.4%. \par \par \uc2  Highlighting other volatile Asian markets, Taiwan's key stock index fell 2.7% after trading resumed for the first time after last Tuesday's earthquake and a financial market holiday Friday. South Korea's Kospi tumbled 4%, Malaysia's KLSE Index dropped 2.1% and Singapore's Straits Times Index ended down 1.4%. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv9r03r47\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
