\par\par \b \uc2  G7 Spotlights BoJ But Keeps  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Prospects Alive \b0\par\par\uc2  By Damian Milverton \par \uc2 773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 1999\par 19:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--For those few dollar bulls who had quietly shorted the yen before this weekend's Group of Seven meeting, persistence might yet pay off. \par \par \uc2  The G7 communique issued after Saturday's gathering of finance ministers and central bankers was initially interpreted as satisfying Japanese demands for expressions of support while falling short of a clear declaration that the yen has overshot in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Such a determination would have sent a clear warning that concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to bring the yen to heel in the short-term. \par \par \uc2  As it was, dollar traders took heart from the G7 statement that "we shared Japan's concern about the potential impact of the yen's appreciation" and pushed the dollar up to 106.40 yen early Monday from late Friday's position around Y104.20. \par \par \uc2  But this move lacked momentum and it fizzled, allowing the yen to hold off further dollar gains and leaving the dollar quoted at Y106.30 midway through U.S. dealings. \par \par \uc2  This limp reaction mirrors the recognition that it is up to Japan to solve the yen dilemma by becoming more creative with monetary policy than in the past. For this to occur, the Bank of Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo will need to move closer to a common strategy. \par \par \uc2  Such an evolution has been resisted by BoJ officials in the past, coveting their independence from an insistent MoF, and any move toward compromise is expected to take time. \par \par \uc2  As such, coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen between the BoJ and its G7 allies is still a very real prospect to those familiar with the history of G7 policy. \par \par \uc2  "We will continue to monitor developments in exchange markets and cooperate as appropriate," the G7 said in a passage that has become a fixture of past communiques. \par \par \uc2  The problem for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists is that this phrase seldom alters to reflect the G7's real intentions. This was the case in 1995 when there was last concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to arrest an earlier steep ascent by the yen, deemed unjustified by underlying economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Further supporting those who suspect concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the weeks ahead is the G7's reference to the yen in their latest communique - a rarity even in past times of financial market volatility. \par \par \uc2  According to the G7 statement Saturday, the yen is of concern for Japan and its partners because of the threat it poses to Japanese recovery and world growth. The G7 remain committed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they believe it necessary. \par \par \uc2  So what might be the trigger for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now? \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        Mildly Positive Signals From \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par    In Washington, there is a feeling that anyintervention might be\par wasted unless underpinned byappropriate monetary policies. Currently,\par Japanesemonetary policy settings are short of what is desired andthe\par BoJ did itself no favors by declaring last week therewould be no\par change in settings in the month ahead.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But in Washington, there appears to have been the subtlest mellowing\par among BoJ officials, expressed with cautious phrasing by BoJ Governor\par Masaru Hayami.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Bank is prepared to use the flexibility it has in the context of\par the present monetary policy stance to respond appropriately and timely\par to developments in the economy as well as financial markets, including\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market," Hayami said Sunday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We are exploring how we could improve money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so as\par to assure the further permeation of liquidity in the context of the zero\par interest rate policy," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This emphasis on wringing better results out of monetary policy stems\par from the BoJ's concern "about the negative impact of the recent rapid\par yen appreciation on economic activity and prices," Hayami said, adding\par "this view is shared with the government."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It is this last remark in particular that is providing some of Japan's\par G7 partners with hope that Japan's two crucial economic policy centers\par may finally be moving toward a consensus approach to the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Should this be the case, it will paradoxically create more favorable\par sentiment within the G7 toward concerted action against a strengthening\par yen just as the market itself is reducing the need for such action by\par bringing the yen down of its own accord.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Such an outcome would sit well with many officials in the G7, who\par viewed the past weekend as reflecting another chapter in reforming\par Japan's rigid economic structures rather than a passing page in the\par history of the global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Damian Milverton; Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9272;\par damian.milverton@dowjones.com