\par\par \b \uc2  =POINT OF VIEW: Not Easy For Japan To Make Policy Shift \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robert Flint \par \uc2 524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 1999\par 20:37\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--This weekend's Group of Seven meeting in Washington left the impression Japan is prepared to make a broad policy response to yen strength, but rapid changes won't come easily. \par \par \uc2  Because of political realities, sweeping economic measures will take the Japanese government months to implement. And despite the conciliatory rhetoric after the G7 meeting, it's by no means sure the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of Japan are seeing eye-to-eye on strategy, much less tactics. \par \par \uc2  Numerous obstacles still stand in the way of giving markets visible proof of a shift in Japanese policy. Without such proof, the effect of the G7 communique and jawboning by G7 officials will have only a short-term effect in slowing yen appreciation. \par \par \uc2  According to many U.S. economists and government officials, the key to ensuring the viability of Japan's economic recovery is increasing private demand. But stimulus measures of this magnitude require passage of government propositions by parliament, which doesn't happen overnight. Once the measures are passed into law, it takes even longer for the impact to show on the economy. \par \par \uc2  In the short run, an increase in Japan's money-market liquidity would be the best method of weakening the yen - far more effective than unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to analysts. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent an estimated $35 billion on repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen during the summer. \par \par \uc2  But the effect was short-lived, as the Bank of Japan mopped up the excess liquidity supplied through invention by selling Treasury bills. As yet, the BOJ hasn't given any concrete indication it is ready to take a more accommodating monetary stance, such as "unsterilized" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which adds liquidity to the market. \par \par \uc2  So far, the BOJ has stubbornly exerted its independence from outside influence. Weekend comments from BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be considered in policy decisions hinted at a shift in policy. \par \par \uc2  But on Monday, the BOJ left its usual Y1.0 trillion in money markets. In the published minutes from its Aug. 13 meeting, the BOJ board clearly indicated it felt increasing money-market liquidity would have no impact on the yen. Nothing in the statements of BOJ officials since shows a substantial change in the BOJ's opinion. \par \par \uc2  Japanese monetary officials, like the yen itself, are trapped in an unenviable position, reminiscent of the characters in Joseph Heller's classic novel "Catch 22." \par \par \uc2  Because Japan's economic recovery is considered well underway, the yen is strengthening. But the yen's strength threatens the recovery because it makes Japanese export products more expensive. \par \par \uc2  Without substantial action and not just words, the yen is bound to continue appreciating against the dollar. That means we'll soon see the dollar test the Y100 level, which would definitely threaten Japan's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par An that is Catch-22 in its purest form.\par    -By Robert Flint; Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-4408\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par e-mail: robert.flint@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  09-27-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1437EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 