\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Miyazawa wins support, has challenge at home. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 1999\par 21:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Sept 27 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa left Washington on Monday after winning verbal support for Tokyo's drive to curb the yen's rise but now faces the challenge at home of meeting Japan's side of the bargain. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa wrung a rare statement from the Group of Seven major industrial nations that specifically voiced its concern over the surging yen's potential to strangle the fragile economic recovery in the world's second-largest economy. \par \par \uc2  In return, Japan pledged on Saturday to do more to stimulate its sluggish economy through spending programmes and its newly independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would look at ways of providing "ample liquidity" for the economy. \par \par \uc2  "It was fine," Miyazawa said to reporters before leaving for Tokyo after talks held on the sidelines of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank meetings. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa's efforts were rewarded by a rise in the dollar against the yen on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, helping to relieve some pressure on Japanese exporters whose goods threatened to be priced out by the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading at around 106.04 against the dollar on Monday compared with around 104.20 yen on Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  A senior Japanese finance ministry official declined to evaluate the dollar's rise against the yen. Refusing to say whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bring the yen down, he said the ministry would continue to monitor developments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carefully. \par \par \uc2  MEETS PRIME MINISTER TUESDAY \par \par \uc2  Once he returns to Tokyo, Miyazawa will have to ensure that Japan persists with its efforts to bolster the economy with spending programmes and hope that the independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes through on its pledge to provide a monetary stimulus. \par \par \uc2  He is scheduled to meet Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi on Tuesday to discuss how to tackle the strong yen and the declining stock market. \par \par \uc2  Obuchi told business leaders he wanted to form a new cabinet by the end of this week to consider a comprehensive package of measures that would aim at economic revival. \par \par \uc2  "The new cabinet will consider a comprehensive package of economic measures to put Japan's economy on a full-fledged recovery track," he told the gathering on Monday. \par \par \uc2  In Washington, Miyazawa said Tokyo will soon decide on the use of 500 billion yen earmarked for public works projects for this fiscal year that ends on March 31, 2000. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government also must start working to compile a supplementary budget worth of 4 trillion to 5 trillion yen to ensure the economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  On the monetary side, the Bank of Japan (BOJ) needs to keep its lax monetary policy in place to help offset the negative effects of the government's spending programmes, which risk pushing up interest rates and strengthening the yen. \par \par \uc2  It has been a sense of a lack of consistency in the policies of the Japanese government and the BOJ that has helped to boost the yen's value against the dollar recently. Financial markets thought the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ignoring the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  BOJ MAKES POLICY CLEAR \par \par \uc2  But over the weekend, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami made it clear that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policies were related to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements. \par \par \uc2  In meetings on Monday, Japan won support from its Asian neighbours for its concern over the yen's rise when Miyazawa met Association of South East Asian Nations (ASEAN) finance ministers in Washington. \par \par \uc2  "Basically we share the same opinion of the G7. If the yen is too strong, it may affect Japan's recovery and that is also not favourable for Asian nations," said Thailand Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda. \par \par \uc2  The ASEAN ministers showed a strong interest in the future course of Japanese economic policy, said the senior Japanese finance ministry official. \par \par \uc2  "Japan told ASEAN nations that it will continue to take economic stimulus measures until domestic-led economic recovery is in place," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9r033qx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
