\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar steps higher, markets still eye Japan moves. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 1999\par 17:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The dollar stepped higher at the start of the week after Japan over the weekend hinted that it may agree to new policies aimed at taming the yen. \par \par \uc2  But investors insisted that policy makers deliver on vague promises before agreeing to reverse the yen's recent sharp gains, setting the stage for a wait and see game that could keep the dollar range-bound for a few more days, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Fuelled by stronger statements from Japan and its partners among the world's seven most industrialised nations, the dollar jumped to around 106 yen overnight, marking a near two yen rise from Friday's New York close. \par \par \uc2  By the time U.S. trading got underway however, the dollar had slipped off those highs to trade around 105.38/46 yen. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, the dollar held small gains against Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leaving the euro to start at $1.0412/15, shy of Friday's $1.0440/45 close. \par \par \uc2  It also inched up against the Swiss franc to 1.5386/94 from 1.5330/40 while the British pound flexed its muscle to start at $1.6465/73, up from $1.6413/23. \par \par \uc2  Even as trading was thin, the market's attention was still squarely focused on the yen and how Japan and its partners would tackle the issue of a rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Over the weekend Japanese officials pledged to investigate ways of permeating the market with more liquidity and spark domestic demand-led growth with more stimulus measures. \par \par \uc2  In return the world's seven most industrialised nations, which include Japan, the United States, Germany, France, Britain, Italy and Canada, expressed their concern about the the negative knock-on effects the rising yen may have. \par \par \uc2  "The G7 statement was stronger than expected but the market is still waiting for further clarification on what Japan will actually do," said Refco Group analyst Mike Malpede, explaining the market's contradictory response over the last hours. \par \par \uc2  Dealers remained on the lookout for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but said the timing may not be quite right yet as leaders may still wait for the outcome of more policy meetings in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a possibility, but it is more on the back of traders' minds than the front of their minds," a dealer said. On Monday, dealers expect to see the dollar drift between 104.50 yen and 106 yen, before possibly making a break higher later in the week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's fate may also hinge on Wall Street's health, dealers said. The Dow Jones industrial average started the week with gains of more than 80 points, suggesting last week's melt-down, sparked by ongoing worries that U.S. interest rates may have to be raised again soon, may have been temporary. \par \par \uc2  "We may be seeing a very small bit of flight into the dollar again," Rick Barnett, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Royal Bank of Canada said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the so-called commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like the Australian dollar and Canadian dollar, bounced higher as gold prices jumped to multi-month highs after Europe put a cap on official sector sales. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Wim Duisenberg announced on Sunday that 15 European central banks had committed themselves to an annual ceiling of 400 metric tons, or 2,000 metric tons over five years, on combined gold sales, including already announced sales plans by Switzerland and Britain. \par \par \uc2  Gold was fixed $11.70 higher in London on Monday morning at $281.70, the highest since May 7, when the UK Treasury announced plans to sell more than half its gold reserves over a number of years. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar jumped to $0.6531/38 from $0.6502/09 while the dollar dipped against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4675/85 from $1.4717/27. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9r032ar\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
