\par\par \b \uc2  WORLD BONDS-Yen turnaround would give temporary joy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Aline van Duyn \par \uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 1999\par 15:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 27 (Reuters) - Monetary easing in Japan aimed at curbing the yen's strength or signs that dollar/yen has stabilised would open the door for a temporary rally in major government bond markets, analysts said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  A Group of Seven statement this weekend expressed concern at the yen's strength, which could halt the nascent Japanese economic recovery, but the yen's retreat was limited by the lack of clear measures which will be taken to prevent a new climb. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said either strong Japanese monetary measures or a stabilising exchange rate would give bond markets a boost. \par \par \uc2  The former would temporarily lower Japanese government bond yields and encourage shopping for higher yields abroad; a steady exchange rate would similarly boost buying of foreign bonds. \par \par \uc2  "If the Japanese money supply was increased substantially it would impact yields in Japan and stop the yen's rise, both would encourage buying of Treasuries (by Japanese investors)," said Luca Jellinek, strategist at Paribas Capital Markets. \par \par \uc2  "It would help most bond markets, and we might see a good month of rallies. But long term it would mean stronger growth and higher inflation, so the positive effect for bonds would be short lived," he said. \par \par \uc2  Most government bond yields have been steadily rising this year as global economic growth triggered a switch towards tightening from easing of monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark U.S. long bond yield has risen from 5.15 percent in January to just less than 6.00 percent. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty about the dollar, which has lost 15 percent against the yen since July, has added to the troubled outlook for U.S. Treasuries. In particular, a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might bring back Japanese buying of higher yielding foreign bonds. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen, but each time it has been unsuccessful. Even concerted G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be unlikely to stem its rise. Easier monetary policy is now seen as key. \par \par \uc2  Finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs of the Group of Seven said in a statement after their meeting on Saturday that they shared Japan's concern about the effect a higher yen might have on Japan's and world economies. \par \par \uc2  Further easing of Japan's already loose monetary policy is seen as a prerequisite for any G7 action, but so far Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has refused to have monetary policy dictated by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  However, Japanese Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi said on Monday he would meet Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa on Tuesday to discuss measures to address the strong yen, among other topics. \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE FLOWS IMPORTANT \par \par \uc2  Ian Douglas, strategist at Warburg Dillon Read, said he would be watching flows out of Japan closely as the start of the second half of the Japanese financial year on October 1 is usually accompanied by strong buying. \par \par \uc2  "Ultimately, much is likely to depend on how sustainable the strong yen is perceived to be by Japanese investors," he said. \par \par \uc2  Jellinek at Paribas said monetary policy in Japan, an easing of which would stimulate the economy, would have to allow for nearly a doubling of money supply growth to 5-6 percent for it to really impact yields. \par \par \uc2  In August, the Japanese money supply average of M2 plus certificates of deposit grew 3.5 percent from a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  "Because money supply is a quantitative measure, size matters in terms of its impact on bond markets. Japan would need to see a big rise in money supply for it to have an impact." \par \par \uc2  He said although the Bank of Japan had resisted such measures in the past, there was now marginally more chance of it happening if G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared only a dim hope. \par \par \uc2  Daniel Moreno, strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson, said if dollar/yen had found a base around the 105 level, this would encourage Japanese investors to buy Treasuries. \par \par \uc2  That, as well as jittery stock markets, might underpin the market, which has been stuck in a bear range since January. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6176, fax +44 171 542 5285, aline.vanduyn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9r031hx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
