\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Gets G7 Pledge Of Concern On Weak Yen, But No More \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 1999\par 13:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--You can't always get want you want. But if you try sometime, you just might find, you can get what you need. \par \par \uc2  At a meeting in Washington Saturday, Japanese officials didn't get an overt promise from their Group of Seven partners on coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen; but they did get an expression of concern about the yen. \par \par \uc2  The communique said in part that the G7 "will continue to monitor developments in exchange markets and cooperate as appropriate." \par \par \uc2  The communique also said that the G7 "shared Japan's concern about the potential impact of the yen's appreciation for the Japanese economy and the world economy." \par \par \uc2  It was the first time in four years that a specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rated mention in a G7 communique, and the fact that G7 financial authorities also pledged to cooperate "as appropriate" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will likely be enough to instill two-risk into dollar-yen trading. \par \par \uc2  Asked if the clause in the communique regarding cooperation was referring to the possibility of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa noted at a press conference that it was common practice for G7 communiques not to specifically mention \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the ways in which it is carried out. \par \par \uc2  "But you can really feel and see that full recognition of the situation has been gained and this has been formally expressed in the communique," Miyazawa said. \par \par \uc2  The situation is this: Despite repeated yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and daily warnings about possible further action from Japanese financial authorities, the yen has remained in a strengthening trend in recent months. The dollar finished New York trading Friday just above Y104. Two months ago, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading above Y120. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government, Finance Ministry and business leaders have become increasingly vocal in decrying the yen's rise in the last few weeks. The concern, they say, is that the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could pull the rug out from under the economy just as it's getting back up on its feet. \par \par \uc2  Significantly, the G7 nations share these concerns. In the communique, the G7 said: "Japan's economy has shown signs of positive growth, although prospects for continued recovery in private demand remain uncertain." \par \par \uc2  With the U.S. running a record trade deficit and Japan's trade surplus still large, notions that Japan still has further to go in ensuring that its economy is on a recovery track could discourage G7 central bankers from risking their credibility by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen for the time being. \par \par \uc2  For that reason, Japan faces the same challenge coming out of the G7 meeting that it faced going in: Loosen both fiscal and monetary policy or prepare for more yen strength. \par \par \uc2  At the meeting in Washington, Japan's list of planned policy steps reportedly didn't go beyond what was already in the works: an extra budget expected to total as much as Y10 trillion from the Finance Ministry and an array of hints from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said that the Bank of Japan would carry out monetary policy with one eye on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ "is prepared to use the flexibility it has in the context of the zero interest rate policy where ample liquidity is provided to respond appropriately and timely to developments in the economy as well as in the financial markets, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. This has been met with the understanding of other countries (present at the meeting)," Hayami told a news confence following the G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ also said that it is exploring how it can improve money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  so as to assure the the further permeation of liquidity in the context of zero interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  These promises, however, aren't likely to excite financial markets or stir the G7 into conducting joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, as they go no further than what's already been factored into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. \par \par \uc2  -By Arran Scott (813-5255-2929;amscott@ap.org) \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 26-09-99 \par \par \uc2  1117GMT \par \par \uc2  1148 4223 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9q072z0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
