\par\par \b \uc2  Japan EPA chief critical of BOJ policies. \b0\par\par\uc2 281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 1999\par 06:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 26 (Reuters) - Japan's Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya said on Sunday that it was probably the wrong decision for the Bank of Japan (BOJ) to maintain its monetary policy on Tuesday following pressure for further easing. \par \par \uc2  Speaking in a TV programme, Sakaiya said the BOJ should have further eased its monetary policy to support Japan's economy, which is showing signs of recovery after a long slump. \par \par \uc2  "The decision by the BOJ's policy board meeting (to maintain its monetary policy) was surprising for me. I think the United States got the same impression as I did," Sakaiya said. \par \par \uc2  The decision "was probably a mistake," he said. \par \par \uc2  Regarding the means of monetary easing, Sakaiya said it is up to BOJ to decide which measures to take. \par \par \uc2  But Sakaiya reiterated that so-called "unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is an effective means for quantitative monetary easing. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been "sterilising" its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by mopping up the extra yen it creates through its recent dollar buying designed to push the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  On the possible effect of further monetary easing on the nation's economy, he said that more ample supply of funds in the money market would lead to lower banks' lending interest rates to companies. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan has argued that by keeping short-term interest rates close to zero, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is doing all it can to pump money into the economy. It has said that any more radical steps, such as unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would create long-term problems. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8018 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9q031w8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
