\par\par \b \uc2  Yen rise seen temporarily tempered after G7. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 1999\par 08:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 26 (Reuters) - A rare Group of Seven (G7) statement expressing concern over the recent sharp rise in the yen is expected to bring short-term relief, but the basic bullish trend for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unchanged, analysts and dealers said on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  The market's attention will now be on Japan's fiscal stimulus plans or extra monetary measures by the Bank of Japan, they said. \par \par \uc2  "With the word 'yen' included in the G7 statement, the market will be careful about extending yen-buying, especially in a provocative manner. This will limit the yen's rise in the near term," said Hitoshi Imamura, deputy manager at Nippon Credit Bank. \par \par \uc2  The G7 communique did not promise joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market but said: "We shared \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's concern about the potential impact of the yen's appreciation for the Japanese economy, and the world economy." \par \par \uc2  It was rare for a G7 statement to mention a specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and even rarer for it to express its concerns. Analysts said it would be enough to stop the yen's rise of about 16 percent since June, at least for now. \par \par \uc2  "With the yen in the communique, I won't be surprised to see active position unwinding of yen long positions, which could lift the dollar towards 107 to 108 yen," Imamura said. The yen closed in New York on Friday at 104.15 yen. \par \par \uc2  The statement also said the G7 nations will continue to monitor developments in exchange markets and cooperate as appropriate. \par \par \uc2  Mitsumaru Kumagai, senior market analyst at the Industrial Bank of Japan, said that Japan probably promised that it will take aggressive economic stimulus measures as a barter to receive support on the yen in the communique. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ did appear to open the door to new measures, as well. Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami for the first time said that exchange rates would be a factor in setting monetary policy, a reversal from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's previous stance, and a bank source said that new measures would be explored to ensure that the "zero-'rate" policy is "fully filtered into the market." \par \par \uc2  "It is still uncertain whether Japan will take additional monetary steps or whether it will maintain its current credit-easing policy. But I got an impression that the G7 members may be seeking more monetary measures," Kumagai said. \par \par \uc2  Growing prospects for confidence in the Japanese economy had encouraged yen-buying since this summer. It rose as high as 103.20 to a dollar on September 15, its highest level since December 1995. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities have tried to slow the rise with single-handed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but with limited success. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said the dollar was unlikely to fall sharply in the near term after the key Nikkei shares average's steep decline last week became a major factor prompting investors to unwind long positions in the yen. \par \par \uc2  One analyst noted that the better Japan does at improving the economy, the worse its yen troubles will become. \par \par \uc2  "You have to remember that fresh measures that work to boost the economy also certainly will work to support the yen in the future," said Takashi Toyahara, deputy manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9q03140\par