\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. dlr gains on G7, market wants words backed up. \b0\par\par\uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 1999\par 01:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar settled around 105.50 yen on Monday morning, having jumped around 1.5 yen at the Australasian opening after G7 finance ministers expressed "concern" over the effects of the Japanese yen's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also marked down against the euro, but traders said the challenge now was for Japanese policy makers to back up the weekend's words with action if Monday's moves were to be more substantial than just a pause in the yen's recent rise. \par \par \uc2  At 2200 GMT (8:00 a.m. Sydney) the U.S. dollar was at 105.50 yen, well up on New York's late 104.12/22 yen. "I think the market is focusing on the fact that there was a comment about the yen specifically in the (G7) communique, which will be supportive in the near term," Jonathon Prince, a senior trader at National Australia Bank said. The G7 communique expressed "concern" at the strength of the yen and its potential detrimental effect on Japanese and world growth, but contained no specific promise of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It also noted that that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would "respond appropriately and timely to developments in the economy as well as financial markets, including \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ". \par \par \uc2  At a briefing in Washington on Sunday, Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami repeated that sentiment and said it would be "inappropriate" for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to target a specific yen exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously the market's is going to want to see some something concrete fairly soon," Prince said. \par \par \uc2  The yen has risen by 15 percent against the U.S. dollar since June, and on September 15 hit a three and half year high of 103.20 yen to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The euro also benefitted against the yen after the G7 statement, rising to 110.08/23 at 2200 GMT, and like the U.S. dollar was expected to be supported, at least initially, as the market digested the implications of the weekend meeting. \par \par \uc2  "This is the first step in the ladder in terms of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They were never going to lay all their cards on the table at once," Mike Symonds, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Bank of New Zealand in Wellington, said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the task now, having seen the yen soften, was for Japanese authorities to manage the policy focus of the Bank of Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  so that, if needed, the G7 nations would be favourably inclined to back up the expression of concern should they continue to strengthen. \par \par \uc2  Elsehwere, the U.S. dollar strength helped the Australian dollar rise towards resistance at $0.6530 in early trade from just under 65 cents late on Friday. The Aussie's gains also helped the New Zealand dollar rise towards $0.5180. \par \par \uc2  ((Sydney newsroon 61-2-9373-1800, sydney.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9q0311n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
