\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP-G7 wins Japan pledge to boost economy further. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Donna Smith \par \uc2 847 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 1999\par 03:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Sept 25 (Reuters) - Major industrial nations extracted a pledge from Japan on Saturday to inject more money into its fragile economy to boost growth and stem the rising yen, which they said was a threat for the world economy. \par \par \uc2  In return for its promise to its Group of Seven (G7) partners, Japan won sympathy, but not much else from a meeting of the group's finance ministers and central bankers. \par \par \uc2  "We shared Japan's concern about the potential impact of the yen's appreciation for the Japanese economy and the world economy," the G7 said in a statement issued after the meeting. \par \par \uc2  It is extremely rare for a G7 statement to mention a specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and even rarer for it to express its concern. But despite those strong words, the group gave no indication itwas willing to join Japan's recent efforts to stop the yen's rise by selling it on the open market, as Tokyo had hoped. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities pledged to "implement stimulus measures until domestic-demand-led growth is solidly in place and, in the context of their zero interest rate policy, to provide ample liquidity until deflationary concerns are dispelled," the G7 said. \par \par \uc2  The outlook for Japan's economy, the second largest in the world, and the impact of the rising yen on its fledgling recovery figured high on the agenda of the G7 - Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the United States. \par \par \uc2  The yen has risen about 14 percent against the dollar since early July, a gain large enough to spark fears it could make Japanese exports too expensive and throw Japan's recovery off track, and threaten the health of the global economy. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN WANTED G7 SUPPORT TO CAP YEN RISE \par \par \uc2  Japan wanted the support of its G7 partners in its battle to tame the high flying yen. But the United States has been reticent about the idea of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and urged Tokyo to go further to reflate its economy and boost domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  Last week the Bank of Japan said it would not inject new liquidity into the economy, something its G7 partners - and the United States in particular - have been calling for vociferously. Japan's commitment to economic stimulus appeared to be a major step toward satisfying its partners' demands. \par \par \uc2  While \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to secure any commitment for joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ambiguity of the G7 statement left the possibility of such a dramatic move open. Italian Treasury Minister Giuliano Amato said the statement did not rule it out. \par \par \uc2  "There could be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Amato told a news briefing following the G7 meeting. "The G7 does not rule out yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but does not state that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take place, nor does it specify timing or amount." \par \par \uc2  But European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not discussed at the meeting. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were not discussed, but the intention is to continue to cooperate as is appropriate," Duisenberg told reporters. \par \par \uc2  G7 PLEDGES TO WATCH \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKETS CLOSELY \par \par \uc2  The G7 statement, falling back on its standard language, said the group was watching \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets developments closely and would cooperate as appropriate. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers declined to go beyond the G7 statement. \par \par \uc2  "A good deal of thought and care went into the words we chose in the (G7) communique, certainly with respect to these issues, and I would not want to try and interpret the words further for you. I think they speak for themselves," Summers said at a news conference following the G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa also declined to go beyond the group's statement. \par \par \uc2  "The G7 statement fully reflects their (common) views on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Miyazawa told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  The group said it saw more stable conditions in the world economy and that it saw signs of renewed growth in many Asian nations which had been battered by the financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  The financial leaders also said that the economies of the European Union were looking brighter, but also urged more structural reforms. \par \par \uc2  Economic growth in Europe and Asia could take some pressure off the United States, which as the world's major engine of growth in the last few years, has seen its trade deficit soar to record highs. Those deficits have unnerved financial markets and have helped fuel the yen's rise against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The G7 statement - which in large parts reflected language recently used by U.S. officials - said economic prospects for major industrial nations had improved. But it added that "we still face a number of challenges" to make the world economy a better and more stable place. \par \par \uc2  The group was joined later in their meeting by Russia's Finance Minister Mikhail Kasyanov who said he had assured the group that Moscow was addressing concerns about flows of capital out of the country. \par \par \uc2  The group urged Russia to crack down on corruption and capital flight to ensure international funds do not go astray. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9q0311b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
