\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's Miyazawa Says U.S. 'Made No Requests' At Washington Meeting \b0\par\par\uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 1999\par 18:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Emerging from a meeting with U.S. officials, Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said Saturday that he explained the steps Japan is taking to boost its economy and that U.S. officials made no requests of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa spoke to reporters after meeting with Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan and Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami. \par \par \uc2  Miyaza said finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations would discuss such issues as the yen's appreciation against the dollar and Japan's monetary policy when they meet later Saturday. "Those are issues for everyone," he said. \par \par \uc2  Although, few observers expect the finance officials to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would like to see the U.S. take action to keep the yen from damaging Japan's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile Saturday, Fuji Bank Chairman Toru Hashimoto called on G-7 finance officials to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , telling reporters that the yen is in "overshoot." Fair value, "reflecting current fundamentals," is between 110 yen and 115 yen to the dollar, Hashimoto said. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday in New York, the dollar was trading at 104.21 yen, up slightly from 103.95 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  "I hope that the market will understand about its overshooting and that it will be corrected," Hashimoto said. "I also hope that the G-7 ministers will agree to concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reverse this overshooting." \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto is an outgoing vice chairman of the Institute of International Finance. His successor on the IIF board, Tasuku Takagaki, chairman of the Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi Ltd. (MBK), concurred with Hashimoto's view of the yen and predicted a sharp correction next week. \par \par \uc2  "I would dare say that if we wait until Monday, or if not Monday then Tuesday, I would not be surprised to see the yen at Y110 (to the dollar)," he said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. position is that it is the Japanese who should take action. Earlier this week, Summers said Japan can't rely on "external stimulus" for a sustained recovery from deep recession at home and that policy instead should focus on encouraging domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  Takagaki said his forecast wasn't necessarily predicated on G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He also made it clear that he was expressing his personal opinion. \par \par \uc2  The G-7 meeting Saturday will also discuss debt relief for poor nations and recent allegations that Russians laundered money through Western banks. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv9p03r0p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
