\par\par \b \uc2  Japan appeals to G7 to help rein in yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 1999\par 22:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Sept 25 (Reuters) - Japan was expected to make a last-ditch appeal to Group of Seven rich nations on Saturday for help in arresting the rise of the yen, after the United States apparently declined to offer support in bilateral talks. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, accompanied by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs Masaru Hayami and Alan Greenspan, met for nearly an hour on Saturday before the G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  Asked after the bilateral talks if the two countries had agreed to work to stem the rising yen, a stern-faced Miyazawa said, "It is an issue for everyone and (Japan and the United States) cannot alone conclude this issue." \par \par \uc2  "We are going to discuss it at the G7 meeting to be held in the afternoon," Miyazawa said, adding that for the moment there was nothing to make further comment on. \par \par \uc2  The G7 comprises Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy Japan and the United States. \par \par \uc2  The yen, which has surged some 13 percent since June, was likely to be high on the agenda at the G7 meeting, with Tokyo expected to seek support to drive it down. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have said the yen's sharp rise is undesirable, not only for Japan's economic recovery but also for the global economy. \par \par \uc2  An Italian Treasury official told reporters the Group of Seven agreed that the strength of the yen is a problem, and that Japan was ready to inject liquidity to boost domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  The official, speaking on condition he was not identified, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also ready to conduct non-sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to help drive the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  Normally when central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, they then move to mop the extra liquidity. Unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - in which there is no such mopping up - has a bigger impact on the economy. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury made very little comment after the meeting. But in his recent comments, Summers has made it fairly clear that the United States did not favour \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as a means of reining in the yen. \par \par \uc2  Summers has repeatedly urged Japan to take all possible steps to achieve domestic demand-led growth, while also repeating that a strong dollar was in the U.S. interest. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether Summers and Greenspan had asked Japan to take further economic steps, including further credit easing, Miyazawa told reporters he received no specific requests at Saturday's bilateral meeting. \par \par \uc2  "I told them my view of what Japan can do from now for its economic recovery," Miyazawa said. \par \par \uc2  In a joint news briefing with Miyazawa, Bank of Japan Governor Hayami just smiled when asked by reporters if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been pressured at the meeting to take a quantitative credit easing. This had been regarded as a precondition for U.S-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday its Policy Board decided to keep its current monetary policy unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Japan posted stunning annualized growth of 8.1 percent in January-March and 0.9 percent growth in the April-June quarter, mainly thanks to the government's pump-priming measures and zero-interest rate policy by the BOJ since February. \par \par \uc2  Policymakers are worried that any sharp rise in the yen above the level of 100 to the dollar could wipe out the emerging bright spots in the economy, as well as the better than 20 percent rise in Tokyo stocks so far this year. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9p030ys\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
