\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -2: Dlr Seen Resuming Downtrend Vs Yen Post G7 \b0\par\par\uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1999\par 07:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The G7 meeting is unlikely to produce any agreement on joint dollar-supportive action, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's abstention from opting for further monetary easing last Tuesday, along with McDonough's comments, lead to this conclusion, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ's inaction especially hurts since it showed a rift between the Finance Ministry and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  over monetary policy," said Katsuaki Tanaka, a trader at Nippon Credit Bank. "How can Japan persuade the U.S. and other G7 members to toe the line when its monetary authorities aren't unified?" \par \par \uc2  If the meeting fails to produce a joint-initiative on the dollar/yen as market players are now expecting, the dollar will resume its downward trend against the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar will head for Y100.00 next week, though an expected further fall of Japan's stock prices will provide temporary resistance around Y102.50, some dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Overseas investors, especially U.S. investors will begin to shift their funds back to dollars out of yen around Y102.50, so the level will provide a temporary resistance," said a dealer at a Japanese city bank. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo traders see U.S. stock prices movements as key to U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "If the Dow Jones (Industrial) Average falls below 10000 points, the U.S. will cooperate with Japan in supporting the dollar," Sanwa's Takami said. "The continuing fall of U.S. stock prices will make investors worldwide nervous about holding the dollar and the U.S. must do something to stop it." \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y110.20, up from Y108.96 in New York late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6454, up from $1.6435 in New York late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Takeshi Takeuchi; 813-5255-2929; ttakeuchi@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 24-09-99 \par \par \uc2  0539GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9o06yr2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
