\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Joint  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  move 60-70% likely-Japan's Suzuki. \b0\par\par\uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1999\par 05:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 24 (Reuters) - A top policy-maker in the junior party in Japan's ruling coalition, Yoshio Suzuki, said on Friday he sees a 60 to 70 percent chance that the United States will agree this weekend to join \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Suzuki, deputy policy chief of the Liberal Party and a former Bank of Japan executive director, said on a Reuters Television programme that regardless of joing action, he expects a "massive" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Suzuki said he sees a high probability of "America taking some kind of joint action" against the yen's rise because he thinks Washington is starting to worry about the dollar's weakness and because the BOJ is already doing all it can to ease monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's effective rate against all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - not just the yen - has begun declining, raising U.S. fears of a triple drop U.S. stock and bond prices and the dollar, Suzuki said. \par \par \uc2  He also said there is a misconception that the Bank of Japan avoided easing credit on Tuesday. The BOJ saiod it would maintain its policy of driving the overnight interest rate to nearly zero by pumping massive funds into the money market. \par \par \uc2  Suzuki said any further funding increase would be "meaningless and ineffective". \par \par \uc2  "This is an ultra-accommodative monetary policy, and they will continue it," he said. \par \par \uc2  The G7 will understand Japan is doing all it can on the fiscal and monetary fronts to stimulate the economy and will at least express support for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he predicted. \par \par \uc2  The United States will agree to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed, even if not right away, Suzuki predicted. \par \par \uc2  In any event, he said, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely make "massive" \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen. Suzuki declined to speculate on the amount or precise timing, saying it would depend on market conditions at the time. \par \par \uc2  He added that policymakers' concern was with the speed of the yen rise, not the dollar/yen rate itself. \par \par \uc2  Suzuki said the Liberals and Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi's Liberal Democratic Party were planning a supplementary budget with 4-5 trillion yen in direct fiscal spending for the fiscal year through next March. \par \par \uc2  Before the extra budget kicks in, the government will spend 500 billion yen already budgeted for public works projects in the October-March quarter, Suzuki said. \par \par \uc2  This will go towards construction on the Kansai and Chubu international airports, expanding Tokyo's Haneda airport, building more high-speed "shinkansen" railways and beefing up telecoms infrastructure, he said. \par \par \uc2  Suzuki noted that the government and coalition parties have pledged to maintain this fiscal year's level of stimulus in the initial 2000/2001 budget, saying this fiscal programme will keep the nascent recovery going until capital spending and other private expenditures take over. ((Deborah Collis, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9o035xg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
