\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian stocks tumble in wake of Dow slide. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hiroko Nakata \par \uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1999\par 06:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 24 (Reuters) - Japanese stocks suffered another steep loss on Friday, depressing shares across Asia, as the rising yen and a 200-point tumble on Wall Street cut deep into sentiment. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Following the previous session's sharpest one day drop for this year, the Nikkei 225 average\tab    dived through the psychologically key 17,000 mark for the first time since June 15.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  By midday, the average was down 517.37 points or three percent at 16,808.4. The market was closed on Thursday for a national holiday. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei has fallen more than six percent in the past two weeks, but is still nearly 25 percent up from the start of 1999. \par \par \uc2  "There are fears nothing will come of the G7 meeting in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Akihiro Naemura, a strategist at Okasan Securities in Tokyo, referring to Saturday's meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in Washington. \par \par \uc2  "Of course, if there is any news, any comments suggesting (an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), the Nikkei should make a strong rebound," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, however, advanced on Friday morning on a series of official comments talking down the yen before Saturday's G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  Those comments included a prediction by a top policymaker in Japan's junior coalition party, Yoshio Suzuki, who said he sees a 60 to 70 percent chance that the United States will agree this weekend to join \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  By 0430 GMT the dollar stood at 105.00 yen, more than a yen higher from 103.83/91 in late New York on Thursday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Hong Kong, the Hang Seng Index\tab   slid 1.88 percent to 12,965.8, hit by losses in Tokyo and Wall Street's slide to its lowest closing level in more than five months on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This is in line with expectations due to Wall Street and Japan," said Howard Gorges, a South China Securities director.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Dow Jones industrials ended Thursday at 10,318.59, down 1.95 percent, its lowest finish since April 9.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Australia's All Ordinaries index\tab    slipped about 1.4 percent to 2,887.3, Singapore's Straits Times Index\tab   lost 2.35 percent to 2,019.8, and New Zealand's NZSE-40 key index\tab    ended at a year low of 2050.1, off 0.67 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The blue chips are suffering the most, especially the banks," a dealer with a Singapore brokerage said. "Some funds are definitely getting out with all the uncertainty on Wall Street."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Malaysia's composite index\tab    retreated 1.66 percent to 695.3, slipping below the psychologically important 700 level for the first time since August 11.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If the Composite Index breaks below 690, I would recommend people to just get out and stay out for the remainder of this year," one local dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Thaland, the sharp drop in regional markets and weakness in Thailand's baht \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , drove Bangkok's benchmark composite SET index \tab   down 3.6 percent to 386.1.\par Political unrest in East Timor and violence in the Indonesian capital weighed on Jakarta stocks, contributing to a 3.38 percent drop the JSX index\tab    to 510.34 by 0430 GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the Japanese stock market, high-technology and electronic shares led the key Nikkei average lower. Fujitsu Ltd , NEC Corp  and Sony Corp all fell in the morning by around four to five percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said those shares had also been hit by steep falls in U.S. shares, particularly their counterpart technology shares.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the slide in Tokyo stocks pushed up Japanese government bond (JGB) cash and futures prices and knocked down yields in early trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9o035t4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
