\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Tokyo closes below 17,000, Asia markets beat. \b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1999\par 09:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 24 (Reuters) - Asian markets took a beating on Friday with Tokyo stocks closing below the key 17,000 level as a stiff drop on Wall Street and worries over the strong yen unnerved investors ahead of a weekend meeting of the G7. \par \par \uc2  The dollar firmed as far as 105.15 yen at one point only to lapse to 104.53 by 0615 GMT, compared to New York's finish of 103.82/91. \par \par \uc2  Traders said short covering had been the main theme as the market took cover in case the meeting of G7 finance ministers this weekend produced a deal on curbing the yen. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's key 225-share Nikkei index ended down 2.6 percent to 16,872 - its first close below the psychologically crucial 17,000 level in over three months. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's blue chip Hang Seng Index was down one percent to 13,089 by 0700 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The key 216th 10-year Japanese government bond yield fell more than 10 basis points in afternoon trade to 1.690 percent. \par \par \uc2  SUMMERS OF DISCONTENT \par \par \uc2  "There are fears nothing will come of the G7 meeting in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Akihiro Naemura, a strategist at Okasan Securities in Tokyo, referring to Saturday's meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in Washington. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei has fallen more than six percent in the past two weeks, but is still nearly 25 percent up from the start of 1999. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers rattled markets and dashed some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  hopes by saying \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recovery remained frail but that it was up to Tokyo, and not the U.S., to fix it. \par \par \uc2  In New York, the Dow Jones industrial average fell over 200 points to close at 10,318.6 - its lowest in more than five months. The Nasdaq saw one of its biggest point losses ever. \par \par \uc2  REGIONALS DEPRESSED \par \par \uc2  Australia's All Ordinaries index was down 1.16 percent to 2893.4. Singapore's Straits Times Index was down 2.4 percent to 2018.2 by 0700 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Korean markets were closed for the mid-autumn moon festival. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's markets remained closed in the wake of a devastating earthquake that has killed more than 2,100 people. \par \par \uc2  New Zealand stocks, burdened by an unexpected 0.3 percent fall in June quarter GDP, touched a year low but the NZSE -40 key index recovered to end just 13 points down at 2,050.10. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's composite index was down 1.7 percent to a six-week low at 695.33, dragged by depressed regional bourses and the government's policy direction. \par \par \uc2  Thai stocks were down sharply on across-the-board selling amid bearish sentiment pressured by the fall in the baht and sharp drops in regional markets and the Dow. Thai stocks were down 4.5 percent to 382.43. \par \par \uc2  Political unrest in East Timor and violence in Jakarta drove the index down 3.6 percent and the rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was tense at 8,800/8,850. \par \par \uc2  Manila's 33-share composite index lost 1.18 percent to end at 2,070.11 on the peso's fall and the steep losses in the Dow Jones Industrials overnight. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9o035mo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
