\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian stocks sharply lower on yen worries. \b0\par\par\uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1999\par 12:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 24 (Reuters) - Asian markets closed sharply lower on Friday with Tokyo ending below the key 17,000 level as a heavy fall on Wall Street overnight and worries over the yen's strength unnerved investors ahead of a weekend G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  The dollar firmed as far as 105.15 yen at one point only to lapse to 104.53 by 0915 GMT, compared to New York's finish of 103.82/91. \par \par \uc2  Traders said short covering was the main theme in case the meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers this weekend produced a deal on curbing the yen. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo's key 225-share Nikkei index\tab    pulled down 2.6 percent to 16,872 - its first close below the psychologically crucial 17,000 level in over three months.\par Hong Kong's blue chip Hang Seng Index\tab   closed 1.38 percent lower at 13,032, off the day's low of 12,931.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The key 216th 10-year Japanese government bond yield fell more than 10 basis points in afternoon trade to 1.690 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SUMMERS OF DISCONTENT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There are fears nothing will come of the G7 meeting in terms of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Akihiro Naemura, a strategist at Okasan Securities in Tokyo, referring to Saturday's meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors in Washington.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nikkei has fallen more than six percent in the past two weeks, but is still nearly 25 percent up from the start of 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Thursday U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers rattled markets and dashed some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hopes by saying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recovery remained frail but that it was up to Tokyo, and not the United States, to fix it.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In New York, the Dow Jones industrial average\tab   fell over 200 points to close at 10,318.6 - its lowest in more than five months. The Nasdaq saw one of its biggest point losses ever.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par REGIONALS DEPRESSED\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Australia's All Ordinaries index\tab    was down 1.2 percent to 2,893.4. Singapore's Straits Times Index\tab   fell 2.39 percent to close at a six-week low of 2,019.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Korean markets were closed for the mid-autumn moon festival.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Taiwan's markets remained closed in the wake of a devastating earthquake that has killed more than 2,100 people.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par New Zealand stocks, burdened by an unexpected 0.3 percent fall in June quarter GDP, touched a year low but the NZSE-40 key index recovered to end just 13 points down at 2,050.1.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Malaysia's composite index\tab    closed down 1.82 percent to 694.22, dragged lower by nervousness in regional markets.\par Thailand's key stocks index\tab    was down sharply to a five-month low on across-the-board selling triggered by the baht's recent decline and the falls on Wall Street. The composite SET index finished off 4.61 percent at 382.01.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Political unrest in East Timor and violence in Jakarta drove the index down 2.01 percent to close at 517.54 and the rupiah \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was tense at 8,575/8,650.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Manila's 33-share composite index\tab   lost 1.18 percent to end at 2,070.11 on the peso's fall and the steep losses in the Dow Jones Industrials overnight.\par Bombay shares bucked the regional trend on speculative buying. The key 30-share Bombay Stock Exchange index\tab     rose 1.38 percent at one point. At 0940 GMT, it was up 0.83 percent at 4,770.9.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9o0316q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
