\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. help on strong yen unlikely at G7 - analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1999\par 19:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 23 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. appear increasingly unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, with Washington unperturbed by the yen's sharp gains and Japan unwilling to make concessions on monetary policy, market analysts said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers has repeated the administration's "strong dollar" mantra almost every day this week, but a lack of action by the Treasury to clip the yen's wings has convinced market players Japan may be on its own as Group of Seven officials meet in Washington this weekend. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not something the U.S. Treasury embraces as a policy option," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics. "The only people who appear enamoured with it are the Japanese." \par \par \uc2  Amid optimism that Japan's economy is on the rebound, the yen has soared 15 percent against the dollar this summer, despite repeated bouts of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The G7 meeting of finance ministers and central bankers may yield some talk, but no concrete action toward coordinated efforts to sap yen strength, Wall Street analysts predicted. \par \par \uc2  "We might get some supportive language, but that's it," said Tomas Jelf, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg, Dillon, Read. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities briefly revived speculation of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action overnight, when top Liberal Party policy maker Yoshio Suzuki said he saw a 60 to 70 percent chance Washington would agree to join \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts. \par \par \uc2  But market analysts remained sceptical, and the dollar hovered around 104 yen early Friday, about a yen above the 3-1/2 year lows hit last week. \par \par \uc2  British Finance Minister Gordon Brown said on Thursday no specific paper on the dollar/yen exchange rate had been submitted for discussion at the meeting. \par \par \uc2  Enlisting U.S. participation in an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would require serious concessions by the Japanese, ones they seem very unlikely to make, market analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have speculated that what the U.S. wants is Japanese monetary policy action, such as "unsterilized" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which the BoJ leaves yen it sells in the market rather than mopping it up in daily market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , effectively easing policy. \par \par \uc2  Another option that would also permanently increase Japan's money supply, dealers said, would be for Japan to monetize its debt by directly buying Japanese government bonds. \par \par \uc2  The BoJ may have missed its big chance to make a deal with the United States earlier this week, when it declined to lower already near-zero interest rates at its latest policy-setting meeting, market analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Talk that the U.S. Treasury might be willing to spend its own money to crimp the yen's ascent if the BoJ were to take monetary policy action had helped push the dollar as much as five yen above last week's multiyear lows near 103 yen. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback deflated almost as quickly after the BoJ refused to budge on the policy front. \par \par \uc2  The BoJ would face serious credibility problems if it caved to outside pressure to shift its monetary policy, said Warburg's Jelf. \par \par \uc2  "It just doesn't look like Japan can deliver what the United States would require," Jelf said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's efforts to enlist Washington's help might become easier, however, if the greenback's increased weakness against the yen spreads into across-the-board dollar declines and seriously destabilises U.S. asset markets, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  For now, the fact that the yen has popped higher against all major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not just the dollar, makes it easier for Washington to say it is Japan's problem to deal with. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's fall has given U.S. stock and bond markets a slight case of the jitters. But the greenback has remained relatively firm against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rising 11 percent against Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the euro's debut in January. \par \par \uc2  While a concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might pack a greater punch, even that might not be able to turn the yen around for long, some Wall Street analysts said. \par \par \uc2  With the yawning U.S. trade deficit, which widened to a record $25.18 billion in July, and the steady flow of assets into Japan pointing to a weaker dollar, Washington and Tokyo would have a difficult time making \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stick, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "I'm very sceptical that any of it is going to change the world much," said \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytic's Gilmore, adding its only a matter of time before the yen surges again. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think its unreasonable to think (the dollar) will get below 100 yen," Gilmore said. \par \par \uc2  ((North American Treasury Desk, 212-859-1607)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters 