\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Economist Urges Japanese Government To Spend More \b0\par\par\uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 1999\par 21:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--A leading Japanese-based economist defended the Japanese government's massive spending programs for the still-troubled economy Thursday, saying more spending is needed to ensure a lasting recovery. \par \par \uc2  "We have to maintain fiscal spending for the time being," said Richard Koo, chief economist at Nomura Research Institute, a research arm of Japan's largest brokerage firm. \par \par \uc2  "We're in a very important turning point, and as long as fiscal stimulus is in place (Japan will) recover in the not-so-distant future," he said. \par \par \uc2  The country's asset markets are beginning to stabilize, moving businesses and individuals closer to paying down older debt and restoring their bruised balance sheets, Koo said in a speech to Japan Society in New York. \par \par \uc2  Koo, a well-known advocator of fiscal stimulus measures, didn't specify how much more spending is needed to put Japan back in a solid recovery path. \par \par \uc2  He also didn't discuss the appropriate size of the extra budget Tokyo will compile for the second half of the current fiscal year. Koo in the past had called for an extra budget of Y10 trillion. \par \par \uc2  Koo also defended the Bank of Japan's decision Tuesday to keep monetary policy unchanged despite calls at home and from abroad that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should adopt looser policy to keep the yen's rise from derailing the country's nascent recovery. \par \par \uc2  "There is very little the Bank of Japan can do," he said. \par \par \uc2  As a result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa is under pressure to promise to other finance ministers a larger extra budget than previously estimated at the weekend Group of Seven leading industrialized nations' gathering. \par \par \uc2  As for speculation the U.S. may agree to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen's strengthening if Miyazawa promises a large-enough fiscal stimulus, Koo said, "My guess would be Larry (Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers) will help" once Japan passes a "substantial fiscal package." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  resisted Tuesday calls to leave its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilized" by not draining excess yen proceeds from the money market. In theory, leaving the funds unsterilized would undermine the value of the yen, making the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  more effective. \par \par \uc2  But Koo called the sterilization argument "totally off the mark," saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already injected so many funds into the market that participants sometimes can't absorb it all. The massive injection of funds has created "vast problems" by deteriorating financial conditions of corporate pension funds, he said. \par \par \uc2  As for the dollar-yen exchange rate, Koo repeated his past remarks that a range between Y105 and Y110 is "the best compromise" for the U.S. and Japan. The yen's appreciation to Y105 has been "net positive" for Japan, Koo said, adding that a positive impact of the large inflow of foreign money into the Japanese stock market "probably outweighed" a negative impact on the export industry. \par \par \uc2  But "the government should do everything it can" to stem the yen's rise beyond current levels, Koo said. \par \par \uc2  Around 1920 GMT, the dollar was trading around Y103.92 after reaching a 44-month low of Y103.20 last week. \par \par \uc2  "The best way to do it is to get the fiscal policy in place," Koo said. \par \par \uc2  -Sonoko Setaishi; 201/938-4148; sonoko.setaishi@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 23-09-99 \par \par \uc2  1933GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9n073jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
