\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Watch: Tokyo To Preach Strong-Yen Gloom At G-7 \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 1999\par 04:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--For years, Japanese officials have been hard pressed to convince their counterparts that the nation's economic woes wouldn't drag the rest of the world into recession. \par \par \uc2  But when financial officials from the Group of Seven gather in Washington later this week, Tokyo will deliver a message of gloom and doom: The recent rise of the yen could knock the wind out of economic recoveries around the world. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sources said Japan will emphasize that the yen's rise will depress business sentiment in Japan, derailing the recovery in private demand. If the nation's recovery falters, then global economic prospects will darken. \par \par \uc2  "We want to strongly emphasize that view," one top official said on condition of anonymity. \par \par \uc2  It's an ironic stance for Japan to take, and one it may have a hard time selling. \par \par \uc2  Compared with last year, economic conditions around the world have taken a sharp turn for the better: Japan's economy has finally started growing again, Asian countries have rebounded from their slump much faster than expected, Europe is showing signs of picking up and the U.S. dreadnought continues to plow ahead. \par \par \uc2  In its World Economic Outlook published Wednesday, the International Monetary Fund upwardly revised its forecast for gross domestic product growth in major industrial nations to 2.6% in 1999, compared with a 1.9% growth projection announced in spring. \par \par \uc2  For Japan, the IMF said it now expects GDP to grow by 1.0% this year. In spring, the IMF forecast a 1.4% contraction. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, improving economic conditions have had an undesired consequence. The rush by investors to buy up Japanese assets has put so much upward pressure on the yen that even massive and repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  haven't curbed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par            Hard To Find Sympathetic Ear\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par To step up the effort to weaken the yen, Tokyo will try hard to\par convince its counterparts in Washington that the threat posed by the\par yen's appreciation is real.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But garnering sympathy from the U.S. won't be easy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. total trade deficit hit a record in July of $25.18 billion.\par Meanwhile, the Bank of Japan decided earlier this week to leave monetary\par policy unchanged.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Both were announced Tuesday, and any requests from Japan for help on\par combatting the strong yen "will not gain much sympathy," said Brian\par Rose, chief economist at Warburg Dillon Read in Tokyo.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. doesn't feel any sense of immediate crisis. Despite the\par dollar's fall against the yen, overall the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains\par relatively steady on a trade-weighted basis. And the dollar's weakness\par against the yen hasn't damped sentiment toward U.S. stock markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Also discouraging the U.S. from committing itself to helping Japan is\par the apparent rift in Tokyo regarding how best to halt the yen's rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the one hand, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has consistently objected to\par the yen's rapid appreciation and has directed the BOJ to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to put a stop to the rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has appeared less averse to developments in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. Although the BOJ has paid lip service to MOF's worries\par - Governor Masaru Hayami has said the yen's rapid rise is bad for the\par economy and prices - the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hasn't taken concrete steps to\par curb the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Such steps could include leaving in the money market yen sold during\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to undermine the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's value or adopting an\par easier monetary policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par           BOJ Watching Prices, Not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By defying widespread pressure and deciding Tuesday to leave policy\par unchanged, the BOJ made it clear that monetary policy is aimed at price\par stability and not manipulating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Among other things on its agenda this week, Japan will also try to\par press for further progress on a plan to provide debt relief to the\par world's poorest nations.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan will push for a multilateral approach in the area of financing\par the debt forgiveness as part of the International Monetary Fund's\par Heavily-Indebted Poor Countries initiative.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another area where Japan will strive to make progress is on\par international financial architectural issues. One initiative Japan\par supports is the creation of a new forum - the GX - and will advocate a\par strong Asian presence in its membership, which will include members of\par the G-7 as well as countries that were stricken by financial crisis in\par recent years.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Arran Scott; 813-5255-2929; amscott@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 23-09-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0239GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1187 4256 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9n07054\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
