\par\par \b \uc2  Review: Shroff: Japan: BOJ 1, MOF 0 \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chester Dawson in the Far Eastern Economic Review published Thursday \par \uc2 467 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 1999\par 02:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The message couldn't have been any clearer: The Japanese government wanted its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to increase the money supply in a bid to halt the yen's recent surge against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  In an unusual public arm-twisting effort, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa made his feelings clear during talks with Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami, just days before a regularly scheduled meeting of the bank's policy board. Other government officials also chimed in by suggesting that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action was a prerequisite for joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. An unsourced, front-page story in Japan's leading business daily reported the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was poised to loosen monetary policy, which bolstered investors' expectations for some concession to the call for easier money. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refused to buckle under the mounting political pressure when its policy board met Sept. 21, saying it had no business manipulating exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  While that earned the wrath of its former masters at the Finance Ministry, the bank emerged from the showdown - one of its first such tests since winning full independence last year - with its reputation intact. The decision caused an immediate jump in the yen and triggered a 600-point, or 3.4%, slide in Tokyo's benchmark stock index Sept. 22. Finance Ministry officials and exporters worry it will now be even harder to stop the yen from reaching - or breaching - Y100 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Despite such concerns, some economists and business leaders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made the right move in holding the line. \par \par \uc2  "Providing additional funds in the short-term money market would not have any appreciable effect" on the economy, says Ron Bevacqua, an economist at Commerz Securities in Tokyo. With interest rates already at record lows and demand for funds slack, boosting the supply of money wouldn't necessarily spur borrowing and speed a recovery. \par \par \uc2  What's more, ING Baring Securities economist Richard Jerram points out that because of the lag between implementing monetary policy and getting results, any further easing would be unlikely to produce any tangible benefits for months. "Central bankers are better policymakers than politicians," he says. \par \par \uc2  Any suggestions that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  flood the market with money also stirs haunting memories of the excess liquidity that helped spawn Japan's ill-fated "bubble economy" of the late 1980s. \par \par \uc2  Still, even if the bank did make the right call, its enlarged ego combined with the ham-fisted tactics of the Finance Ministry may only create further volatility in the markets. \par \par \uc2  "It creates policy uncertainty and that's bad for sentiment," says Bevacqua. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 23-09-99 \par \par \uc2  0006GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9n06zvp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
