\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP-No sign of U.S. help on Japan's rampant yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 771 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1999\par 01:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Sept 23 (Reuters) - The United States urged Japan on Thursday to use whatever means it can to boost its wobbly economy from within and gave no indication at all that Washington is willing to help Japan tame the rampant yen. \par \par \uc2  Speaking ahead of a key weekend meeting of finance officials from the Group of Seven major industrial nations, U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said Japan's recovery remained frail and its economic future uncertain - but the onus to do something about that was firmly on Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "There have been some indications that policies put in place have had effect, but certainly there is a continuing challenge of establishing a basis for sustained, domestic demand-led growth," Summers told a news conference in what was his most elaborate comment on the Japanese economy in weeks. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese commitment to continued stimulus, using all available tools, will be critical until a solid, self-sustaining recovery ... is firmly entrenched," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese yen has risen strongly against the dollar on signs of stronger-than-expected growth in the ailing Japanese economy, prompting fears that it could undermine an upturn in the world's No. 2 economy by hurting its export industry. \par \par \uc2  Japan has pleaded for help from other G7 nations in its quest to rein in the yen by selling it on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and the issue is expected to top the weekend's agenda. But Washington has indicated repeatedly that it sees little reason to join any coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. \par \par \uc2  FOCUS ON DOMESTIC GROWTH, SUMMERS URGES \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Instead, U.S. officials have urged Japan to boost its economy through other means, in particular through using fiscal and monetary policies to stimulate consumer demand. But Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this week refused to heed such calls, prompting grumbles of disappointment among its G7 partners. \par \par \uc2  In line with previous practice, Summers declined to answer directly questions about the prospect for joint U.S./Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But he insisted \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should concentrate first on increasing domestic growth. \par \par \uc2  "The balance of risks in Japan continues very much to point to the need for policy to promote expansion in Japan," he said. "Inadequate growth or slowing down remains very much the greater risk to policy and it has to be a concern." \par \par \uc2  While the dollar has tumbled some 14 percent against the yen since July, Summers has steadfastly repeated the U.S. administration's litany that a strong dollar is good for the U.S. economy because it helps to keep inflation down. \par \par \uc2  "A strong dollar is in the national interest of the United States," Summers repeated at the news conference. "Treasury policy has been constant and will be constant." \par \par \uc2  He has insisted that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values should be determined on the basis of economic fundamentals and that governments should not meddle in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. administration's hands-off position on the dollar - a centrepiece of its international economic strategy - has been bolstered by the fact that the greenback is holding up fairly well against other key \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notably the euro. \par \par \uc2  MARKETS DRIVE DOWN DOLLAR FURTHER \par \par \uc2  But markets on Thursday reacted by further driving down the dollar's value, reading Summers' remarks as holding out little promise of U.S. action on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  front any time soon. \par \par \uc2  "Summers seemed to downplay the need for any sort of coordinated action of any kind, which has the market thinking that if Japan wants the dollar supported against the yen they have to do it on their own," said John Beerling, vice president at Norwest Bank. \par \par \uc2  Summers was upbeat on the future for the U.S. economy, now well into the eighth year of an uninterrupted growth streak, and said the global economy as a whole looked better than it did a year ago. \par \par \uc2  But he warned that greater balance in the world economy was a key requirement for keeping the U.S. economy strong. "It would be desirable for there to be more balanced growth globally," Summers said. \par \par \uc2  He also laid out an ambitious agenda to improve the policing powers of the International Monetary Fund by enabling it to better guard against theft and diversion of aid money. \par \par \uc2  In the wake of allegations that Russia had misused billions of dollars of international loans, Summers said the IMF should routinely require external audits of recipient central banks. \par \par \uc2  "Whenever the IMF provides finance in an environment where there is a risk that those funds will be misused, it should require external audits," he said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9n035uk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
