\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's August Trade Surplus Fell 23% While Surplus With U.S. Rose \b0\par\par\uc2 907 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1999\par 04:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's merchandise trade surplus with the world fell almost 23% in August from the same month last year, the government said Wednesday. However, Japan's politically sensitive trade surplus with the U.S. rose more than 24% despite a sharp rise in the value of the yen. \par \par \uc2  Japan posted a trade surplus, the measure of all goods exported by Japan minus those imported, of 689.4 billion yen ($6.57 billion) in August, down 22.6% from the 890.97 billion yen surplus in the year-earlier month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  August's unadjusted surplus, which is measured as goods pass customs but before adjustment for seasonal factors, was in line with an average forecast of 688.2 billion yen by economists surveyed by Dow Jones. \par \par \uc2  Japan's politically sensitive trade surplus with the U.S. rose 24.3% to 534.4 billion yen in August from the year-earlier month. In July, the surplus rose 8% from the same month a year earlier, while in June it was up 18.2%. \par \par \uc2  Japan's trade balance with the U.S. increased for the fourth-straight month in August, as imports fell at a faster pace than exports. Imports from the U.S. fell 19.9% on year in August, while exports declined 4.2%. \par \par \uc2  Japan, fighting to emerge from its worst downturn in 50 years, has been under heavy pressure from the U.S. and other trading partners to rein in the surplus by bolstering its weak economy to spark demand for imports. Japan's economy seems to be on the road to recovery with recent positive gross domestic product data. \par \par \uc2  August marked the fifth-straight month of year-on-year decline in the overall surplus. It fell 4.6% in July, which followed declines of 2.9% in June, 31.5% in May and 12.6% in April. In fiscal 1998, Japan's overall trade surplus rose 23% from the previous year to reach the second-highest total ever recorded. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that the trade gap with the U.S. could emerge as a sticking point when financial authorities from the Group of Seven gather in Washington this weekend. The rate of expansion picked up from rises of 8% and 18.2% in July and June respectively. \par \par \uc2  Coming after the U.S. reported a record trade deficit for July, Wednesday's data cast the spotlight on one of the main culprits behind global trade imbalances: weak domestic demand in Japan. \par \par \uc2  Japan's soaring trade surplus with the U.S. "doesn't look good" said Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said Tuesday that "the (U.S.) trade deficit is a problem for what it means for the manufacturing sector of our economy, the disruptions it creates, and the questions about long-run sustainability that it creates." \par \par \uc2  Analysts speculated that Japan's widening trade surplus with the U.S. could give U.S. officials one more reason not to commit to conducting coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The widening surplus with the U.S. reflects the continuing strength of the American economy, in spite of a fall in the dollar-yen rate," said Brian Rose, chief economist at Warburg Dillon Read in Tokyo. "For this reason it will be very hard to get Japan's trade surplus with the U.S. down ... it would take a huge move in the dollar-yen rate to reduce the surplus," he added. \par \par \uc2  With recent domestic attempts at reversing the yen's rise largely ineffective, Japan is widely expected to ask for international assistance at the G-7 meeting this weekend. \par \par \uc2  Given the record U.S. trade deficit with the rest of the world in July of $25.18 billion and the Bank of Japan's decision to leave monetary policy unchanged, both announced Tuesday, Japan's request "will not gain much sympathy," Warburg's Rose said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, a Japanese official said the ministry was "closely watching" whether the trade balance would continue to shrink. \par \par \uc2  Among the factors that could affect the trade balance are movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, the price of oil, and the strength of the domestic economy, he said. \par \par \uc2  "A stronger yen tends to depress exports and lower the price of imports," the official said, adding that it wasn't clear whether such effects would occur. The dollar traded at an average of 115.91 yen in August, down sharply from an average of 143.48 yen in the year-earlier month, the ministry said. \par \par \uc2  Crude-oil prices were higher in August compared with the previous year levels. Crude oil was $18.5 per barrel in August, up from $13.1 a year earlier, the official said. \par \par \uc2  "The margin of declines in imports (from the U.S.) is still very large," the official said. \par \par \uc2  Helping to depress overall imports from the U.S. were large declines in imports of airplanes and office equipment, the ministry said. \par \par \uc2  A large drop in steel exports was a major factor behind the decline in overall exports to the U.S. The volume of steel exports fell 69.4% in August from a year earlier, mainly because of a negative bounce from high levels last year, the official said. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv9m03qyd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
