\par\par \b \uc2  New-Style G7 Expected To Say Yen Is Overshooting \b0\par\par\uc2  By David G. Pearson \par \uc2 1,136 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 1999\par 00:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  PARIS (Dow Jones)--You don't have to be psychic to guess the main agenda item at next Saturday's G7 meeting in Washington. \par \par \uc2  With the yen making eyes at Y100 to the dollar, the phones lines of G7 finance ministers and central bankers have been humming all week with talk of what to do about the yen's inexorable rise and the apparent policy muddle in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "Exchange rates will be at the center of the G7 meeting given the yen's recent strength," a senior German Finance Ministry official confirmed Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Saturday afternoon's gathering is shaping up as important as the handful of others where the G7 felt they had to give a tug on the tiller to get the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market back on course. \par \par \uc2  While there are fundamental differences in the present situation compared to the yen's surge in 1995, students of history will recall that we were pretty much in the same situation back then. \par \par \uc2  Market operators might, therefore, be well-advised to dust off their collection of G7 statements and remind themselves what the ministers and central bankers said on April 25, 1995: \par \par \uc2  "The ministers and governors expressed concerns about recent developments in exchange markets. They agreed that recent movements have gone beyond the levels justified by underlying economic conditions in the major countries. They also agreed that an orderly reversal of those movements is desirable...". They then pledged "to continue to cooperate closely in exchange markets." \par \par \uc2  Market participants remember what followed. The U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  battered the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with successive barrages of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing the yen back down to more manageable levels. \par \par \uc2  The pendulum swung the other way in 1998, forcing another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the G7, this time in the other direction, to prop up an excessively weak yen. \par \par \uc2  "We are exactly in the same situation" as in 1995, commented a senior G7 monetary official, speaking on condition of strict anonymity. \par \par \uc2  He referred to the "roller coaster" of the dollar/yen relationship over the years that is starting to exasperate policy makers because repeated overshooting makes it more difficult for the Japanese to decide on - and implement - proper fiscal and monetary policies. The yen's gyrations have provoked massive international capital flows that ricochet on interest rates in other countries, so it's not purely a Japanese problem, he added. \par \par \uc2  Although this official wouldn't comment on whether the statement to be released on Saturday will contain similar language to the one in 1995, it's clear he's been thinking about the last time the G7 had to deal with a misaligned \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    So Far, U.S. Officials Remain Aloof\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par To be sure, the noises coming from the U.S. have been somewhat\par predictable, with a Federal Reserve Board member stating bluntly that\par the problem is one of a strong yen and not one of a weak dollar. That's\par evident with the euro at record lows against the yen, and still\par floundering around $104. Back in 1995, on the other hand, the dollar was\par also weak against the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par So far, U.S. Treasury officials have publicly refrained from entering\par the debate. While the Japanese call for assistance has reached a clamor\par in recent days, U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers remains aloof.\par He has faithfully adhered to a line familiar within financial markets\par that a strong dollar is in the national interest and has turned a cold\par shoulder to any queries on potential changes in U.S. attitudes toward\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. view, shared by most other G7 members, is that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par problem will solve itself if sound policies are implemented.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Treasury Secretaries are no strangers to facing down their\par counterparts from Japan and Europe over issues of substance and Summers\par is unlikely to flinch from a fight, even though the pressure for some\par kind of support for Japan - particularly given it has grudgingly adhered\par to U.S. urgings on economic reforms - is as intense as at perhaps any\par other time in recent years.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, the Bank of Japan's dogged determination to defy domestic and\par international calls to loosen monetary policy further will doubtless put\par a damper on any cooperation on exchange rates among the G7, observed a\par European official.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The challenge will be to come up with a convincing formula\par demonstrating to markets that Japan has the support of its G7 partners,\par he noted.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    ECB To Represent French, Italian And German Central Bks\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The next G7 meeting will also mark a turning point from an\par institutional point of view.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Last June in Frankfurt it was agreed that, henceforth, the European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would represent the central banks of France, Germany and\par Italy when the G7 officials discuss monetary policy issues as part of\par the regular surveillance of the G7 economies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par They also agreed that the 11 euro-zone countries would be represented\par by the revolving chairman of the euro-zone finance ministers, currently\par Finnish Finance Minister Sauli Niinisto.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Officials said it's not immediately clear whether or not the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors of France, Germany and Italy will be asked to vacate the\par room when exchange rates are discussed, leaving the U.S., Japan, the\par U.K., Canada and the two European officials to deal with these\par politically sensitive issues.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There's no track record," commented one official, noting that the G7\par since its inception has been an ad-hoc, informal body.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other topics to be broached by the ministers and central bankers will\par be the now traditional discussion of the situation in Russia. Here too,\par there may be some fireworks when the Russian officials get wind of G7\par plans to raise the ante of new financial aid.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In light of recent allegations that Russia misused part of its foreign\par aid, the IMF will draw up a "catalogue of additional conditions and\par demands" Russia will have to meet in order to qualify for further\par financial assistance, according to the German Finance Ministry.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the G7's admonishments and threats of withholding future\par assistance may not carry much weight. Western officials acknowledge\par that, whatever happens, the G7 would never provoke a situation that\par creates political upheaval inside Russia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other topics that will be raised on Saturday, time permitting, include\par institutional reform of the International Monetary Fund, progress in\par strengthening the global financial system, and the creation of a new\par group, the G20, that would associate the G7 and certain other\par middle-income countries in studying ways to foster stability in the\par world's markets. The new group will supplant the so-called G22.\par -By David Pearson - Dow Jones Newswires; (33)140171740;\par david.pearson@dowjones.com\par    -Damian Milverton contributed to this article\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 23-09-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2235GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9m06zqh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
