\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Trade Surplus Down In August; Rises 24% With U.S. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nick Shindo \par \uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1999\par 05:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)-- Japan's trade surplus with the rest of the world slumped almost 23% in August from the same month last year, but its politically sensitive trade surplus with the U.S. rose more than 24% despite a sharp rise in the value of the yen. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that the nation's overall merchandise trade surplus, the measure of all goods exported by Japan minus those imported before adjustment for seasonal factors, declined 22.6% to Y689.4 billion in August. The surplus was in line with expectations of economists in Tokyo, who forecast on average that the surplus would dip to Y688.2 billion. \par \par \uc2  August marked the fifth straight month of year-on-year declines in the overall surplus, and the rate of contraction accelerated after falls of 4.6% in July and 2.9% in June. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that the trade gap with the U.S. could emerge as a sticking point when financial authorities from the Group of Seven gather in Washington this weekend. The rate of expansion picked up from rises of 8% and 18.2% in July and June respectively. \par \par \uc2  Coming a day after the U.S. reported a record trade deficit for July, Wednesday's data threw the spotlight on one of the main culprits behind global trade imbalances: weak domestic demand in Japan. \par \par \uc2  Japan's soaring trade surplus with the U.S. "doesn't look good" said Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said Tuesday that "the (U.S.) trade deficit is a problem for what it means for the manufacturing sector of our economy, the disruptions it creates, and the questions about long-run sustainability that it creates." \par \par \uc2  Analysts speculated that Japan's widening trade surplus with the U.S. could give U.S. officials one more reason not to commit to conducting coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  Illustrating the weakness of domestic demand in Japan, overall imports in August dropped 2.4% on year, the 20th successive month of declines. Imports from the U.S. fell 19.9% on year, despite a 19% strengthening of the yen from levels a year earlier. In theory, the strong yen undermines Japanese exports and supports imports. \par \par \uc2  "The widening surplus with the U.S. reflects the continuing strength of the American economy, in spite of a fall in the dollar-yen rate," said Brian Rose, chief economist at Warburg Dillon Read in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "For this reason it will be very hard to get Japan's trade surplus with the U.S. down...it would take a huge move in the dollar-yen rate to reduce the surplus," he added. \par \par \uc2  With recent domestic attempts at reversing the yen's rise largely ineffective, Japan is widely expected to ask for international assistance at the G7 meeting this weekend. \par \par \uc2  Given the record U.S. trade deficit with the rest of the world in July of $25.18 billion and the Bank of Japan's decision to leave monetary policy unchanged, both announced Tuesday, Japan's request "will not gain much sympathy," Warburg's Rose said. \par \par \uc2  Although Japan's economy seems to be on the road to recovery with recent positive gross domestic product data, as domestic demand is still in a slump, Japan is under intense pressure from the U.S. and other trading partners to do more to rein in the surplus and stimulate imports. \par \par \uc2  Trade with Asia remained buoyant, with a 3.0% increase in exports and 4.5% rise in imports, largely a result of the recovery in regional economies, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The overall Japan trade surplus is expected to remain steady for the rest of the year as exports to Asia and imports continue to improve, analysts said. "However, this hinges on signs of recovery not fizzling out," Rose said. \par \par \uc2  Importantly, however, the trade surplus with the U.S. could well increase further even if the dollar-yen rate continues to weaken as international trade typically lags 6 months behind \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  -By Nick Shindo (813-5255-2929;nshindo@ap.org) \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 22-09-99 \par \par \uc2  0327GMT \par \par \uc2  1137 4204 62551 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9m06x1t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
