\par\par \b \uc2  Miyazawa - No need to increase planned fiscal steps. \b0\par\par\uc2 284 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1999\par 12:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Wednesday that Japan does not need to increase the volume of fiscal spending it has considered in compiling a supplementary budget for the fiscal year to next March. \par \par \uc2  Asked if Japan will be asked to increase fiscal spending at a weekend meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers in Washington, Miyazawa told a news conference it was unlikely, "since we have already said that such steps will eventually be necessary". \par \par \uc2  The question arises due to the Bank of Japan's decision on Wednesday not to make a quantitative monetary easing. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been pressured to do so to help stem the yen's rise, and the step was considered a precondition for the United States to join \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa repeated that it was understandable that an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have different views from the government on monetary policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It is up to the BOJ and not a matter for debate," Miyazawa said. \par \par \uc2  Asked if the BOJ decision will make it difficult for Japan to win U.S. support for reining in the yen at the G7 meeting, Miyazawa said: "I have no idea until I get there". \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said he did not think there was a big difference between Japan and the United States in their basic views on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Regarding the more specific topic of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the United States, he said: "You need to have common views when \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9m03at9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
