\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. stocks end broadly mixed, confront bad news. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Melville \par \uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1999\par 22:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Stocks ended broadly mixed onWednesday after the market confronted a slew of uncertainties, including the dollar's continued weakness, a widening U.S. trade gap and the prospect of another increase in U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average ended down 74.40 points, or 0.70 percent, at 10,524.07, a day after tumbling 225 points to post its biggest drop in points since May 27. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq composite index rose 37.06 points, or 1.31 percent, at 2,858.16, thanks to strength in technology stocks. \par \par \uc2  In the broader market, declining issues led advances 1,721 to 1,216 on active volume of 808 million shares on the New York Stock Exchange. \par \par \uc2  "What we see in this market is a parade of horribles," said Alan Ackerman, market strategist at Fahnestock & Co. \par \par \uc2  "There is concern about the trade gap, concern about weakness in the dollar, concern over interest rates, and general weakness underlying on the technical side - decliners continue to lead advancers," Ackerman said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the stock market was approaching key technical support levels. Concern focused on the Standard & Poor's 500-stock index, which slipped briefly below the 1,300-point support level, then rallied. \par \par \uc2  Greg Nie, a technical analyst at Everen Securities Inc. in Chicago, called the drop a "warning flag." The gauge has not closed below 1,300 since Aug. 12. \par \par \uc2  A solid drop through 1,300 could trigger a new round of selling. \par \par \uc2  The S&P 500 index ended up 2.93 points, or 0.22 percent, at 1,310.51. \par \par \uc2  Despite the late recovery, analysts said it was unlikely the market's woes were over, linking much of the late buying to seasonal buying activity by mutual funds. \par \par \uc2  "Technology has been the standout group this quarter and right now we're near the end of the quarter, which means funds are putting their money to work," said Scott Bleier, chief investment strategist at Prime Charter Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Bleier pointed to large Internet names like Yahoo Inc. and America Online Inc. as well as regional telephone companies like Bell Atlantic Corp. as benefiting from the quarterly buying, commonly known on Wall Street as "window dressing." \par \par \uc2  AOL shares jumped 7-1/8 to 91-3/8, Yahoo gained 9-15/16 to 179-1/2 and Bell Atlantic rose 2-9/16 to 67-1/8. \par \par \uc2  The dollar briefly slid below 104 yen, trading at 103.9, down from Tuesday's close of 104.8. On Monday, it hovered at 106. \par \par \uc2  The consensus is that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will need to ease its monetary policy in an effort to prevent its frail economy from slipping back into recession. U.S. officials fear the rest of Asia could feel domino effects just as Japan is starting to climb out of a deep recession. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  refused to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to boost the yen and it also made only a token move to pump more money into the economy, bucking international pressures to lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, tobacco stocks were in the news as the U.S. Justice Department filed its long-expected suit against cigarette makers. The federal government said it will seek to recover much of the $20 billion it spends every year on smoking-related costs. \par \par \uc2  Shares in the world's largest tobacco company, Philip Morris Cos. Inc., also one of the 30 stocks that make up the Dow average, fell 1-1/8 to 34-1/2 after it said it will mount a vigorous defence to the suit. \par \par \uc2  Other tobacco stocks were also lower. R.J. Reynolds Tobacco Holdings Inc. was down 1-3/16 at 27-5/16, and Loews Corp., which operates Lorillard Tobacco Co., lost 1-15/16 to 71-11/16. \par \par \uc2  Copper companies' stocks rallied as industry leader Phelps Dodge Corp. raised its hostile bid for merging rivals Asarco Inc. and Cyprus Amax Minerals Co. to $2.77 billion in cash and stock. \par \par \uc2  Phelps Dodge shares gained 1-1/2 to 58-11/16; Asarco added 2-1/16 to 22-1/2 and Cyprus Amax rose 1-7/16 to 18-13/16. \par \par \uc2  Xerox Corp. rose 7/16 to 42-7/16 after the company agreed to buy Tektronix Inc.'s colour printing unit for $950 million in cash, a deal aimed at helping Xerox shift from dependence on copiers, as well as to enable it to challenge office-printing leader Hewlett-Packard Co. \par \par \uc2  Tektronix rose 1-3/8 to 36-3/16. Hewlett-Packard fell 7/8 to 98-1/2. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9m039ta\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
