\par\par \b \uc2  Wall St jolt sees dollar weakness seep beyond yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1999\par 13:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Wall Street's two percent tumble late on Tuesday is shifting the focus of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets from the yen's inexorable climb back to the domestic clouds gathering over the U.S. dollar, analysts said on Wednesday. Concern about the ballooning U.S. balance of payments deficit and the risks from overstretched U.S. stocks will continue to haunt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into year-end, even if caution ahead of the weekend meeting of the Group of Seven richest nations has dampened the impact for now, they said. \par \par \uc2  News on Tuesday of a record $25.18 billion trade deficit in July was the latest trigger for dollar jitters. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to 1-1/2-week highs against the dollar above $1.05, while the dollar dropped to within a yen of last week's 3-1/2-year lows around 103.20 yen after the Dow Jones Industrial Average recorded one of its biggest losses this year. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also fell to its lowest level against the British pound since May. \par \par \uc2  "One of the reasons why the dollar has fallen in the past couple of months is concern about the current account deficit and about the Fed raising interest rates and slowing growth," said David Brickman, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at PaineWebber in London. "There is no reason for that to change." \par \par \uc2  The focus for the dollar's losses in recent months has been the dollar/yen exchange rate, but the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained generally resilient against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England's trade weighted index for the dollar has shed less than five percent from a 1999 peak of 104.88 hit in July, while it has erased more than 16 percent against the yen over that period. \par \par \uc2  EYEING \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  The dollar's short-term direction hinges on whether the U.S. Federal Reserve will join the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the soaring yen's strength, following a meeting on September 25 of G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Traders are sitting on their hands until then. \par \par \uc2  "There is always a slight risk ahead of G7 - it's a good excuse to do nothing and wait for some more information," said Andrew Milligan, director of economic research at Morley Fund Management. \par \par \uc2  But Milligan, along with a majority of economists polled by Reuters last week, expected no joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take place, allowing the dollar to fall further, towards 100 yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would give investors time to look at domestic concerns for the dollar, which in turn was likely to feed into a stronger euro. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar doesn't look like it's free-falling against the yen any more, but it certainly doesn't look like it's going up either, given the weakness in U.S. asset markets," said Junpei Yamamoto, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi, who saw both euro and Swiss franc rising against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Brickman at PaineWebber also saw U.S. weakness and relative euro-zone strength underpinning the euro against the dollar into 2000. \par \par \uc2  "Demand for commodities priced in dollars will give a technical reason for dollar support into the year-end," said Brickman. \par \par \uc2  "But there will be a recovery in the German economy and the euro will be the strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of euro, dollar and yen into next year." \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets +44 171 542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010914dv9m00ijb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
