\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Soaring yen jumps BOJ hurdle. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deepthi Wickremasinghe \par \uc2 1,063 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1999\par 17:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 21 (Reuters) - The yen kept a firm grasp of its gains of more than three yen against both the dollar and the euro on Tuesday and higher levels were expected as prospects of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength faded. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement earlier of no change in its monetary policy, followed by a record U.S. trade deficit muted expectations that any kind of joint action would emerge at the meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  "Today was the key event. G7 will have very little impact on changing this trend in dollar/yen, so new lows should be simply a matter of time and we will soon be staring at double digits," said Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly the BOJ are not intent on easing monetary policy, there is still a lot of investment demand for the yen and that demand will drive the yen higher." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 104.14/19 yen by 1431 GMT, down three percent from an overnight high of 107.35 and compared with a 106.33/42 late in Europe on Monday. So far on Tuesday the dollar has traded as low as 103.80, just over half a percentage point from its 3-1/2 year lows set on September 5 at 113.17. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's announcement was followed by comments from the Bank's Governor Masaru Hayami that the BOJ would continue to use interest rates as its main monetary policy means and that an easing effect could not be expected from injecting additional funds now. \par \par \uc2  "Hayami has decided not to bow to pressure from the Finance Ministry and from politicians to do something with the money supply," said Steven Saywell, treasury analyst at Fuji Bank in London. "The market will now be testing the limits of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the BOJ and pushing dollar/yen lower." \par \par \uc2  He said the BOJ's decision coupled with a record U.S. trade deficit made it unlikely the U.S. would support the BOJ in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S. international trade deficit for July came in at $25.18 billion compared with $24.60 billion in June and was seen adding to the dollar's soft tone. \par \par \uc2  Meggyesi said the U.S. data supported the view that the dollar is overvalued and that at some point the increase in the deficit would become unsustainable. \par \par \uc2  "(That would) put downward pressure on the dollar through a growing reluctance on the part of foreign investors to continue to finance the deficit," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors are the world's biggest supplier of international capital. Going forward they will be reluctant to buy foreign assets and that will put pressure on countries that have big current account deficits, primarily the U.S." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. data helped the euro to rally briefly to a 1-1/2 week peak against the dollar at $1.05. \par \par \uc2  The euro has since drifted off that high and was last at $1.0465/68 compared with $1.0405/07 late in Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was boosted earlier in Europe by a stronger-than-expected west German August Ifo business climate index which came in at 95.3, its highest since August 1998, but the euro later traded down to a day's low of $1.0364. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is struggling to push ahead," said Meggyesi at Deutsche Bank, adding that many fund managers were already fairly long of euros, so there was limited marginal demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It will be a fairly protracted recovery if that is indeed what we get in the euro," he said. \par \par \uc2  ((London capital markets, +44 171 542 7792, fax +44 171 542 5285, deepthi.wickremasinghe@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's announcement was followed by comments from the Bank's Governor Masaru Hayami that the BOJ would continue to use interest rates as its main monetary policy means and that an easing effect could not be expected from injecting additional funds now. \par \par \uc2  "Hayami has decided not to bow to pressure from the Finance Ministry and from politicians to do something with the money supply," said Steven Saywell, treasury analyst at Fuji Bank in London. "The market will now be testing the limits of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the BOJ and pushing dollar/yen lower." \par \par \uc2  He said the BOJ's decision coupled with a record U.S. trade deficit made it unlikely the U.S. would support the BOJ in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S. international trade deficit for July came in at $25.18 billion compared with $24.60 billion in June and was seen adding to the dollar's soft tone. \par \par \uc2  Meggyesi said the U.S. data supported the view that the dollar is overvalued and that at some point the increase in the deficit would become unsustainable. \par \par \uc2  "(That would) put downward pressure on the dollar through a growing reluctance on the part of foreign investors to continue to finance the deficit," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese investors are the world's biggest supplier of international capital. Going forward they will be reluctant to buy foreign assets and that will put pressure on countries that have big current account deficits, primarily the U.S." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. data helped the euro to rally briefly to a 1-1/2 week peak against the dollar at $1.05. \par \par \uc2  The euro has since drifted off that high and was last at $1.0465/68 compared with $1.0405/07 late in Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was boosted earlier in Europe by a stronger-than-expected west German August Ifo business climate index which came in at 95.3, its highest since August 1998, but the euro later traded down to a day's low of $1.0364. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is struggling to push ahead," said Meggyesi at Deutsche Bank, adding that many fund managers were already fairly long of euros, so there was limited marginal demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It will be a fairly protracted recovery if that is indeed what we get in the euro," he said. \par \par \uc2  ((London capital markets, +44 171 542 7792, fax +44 171 542 5285, deepthi.wickremasinghe@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9l00cmw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
