\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-BOJ has little room to ease-Nagashima. \b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1999\par 08:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 21 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan has little room to further ease credit but could increase the fund surplus in the Tokyo money market through flexible market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , former senior BOJ official Akira Nagashima said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to Reuters while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Policy Board was holding a monetary policy meeting, Nagashima, an adviser to Tokio Marine & Fire Insurance Co, also said a failure to reach a U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  deal on joint yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  could knock the dollar below its recent low of 103 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The principle of the BOJ's monetary policy should not be distorted by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy" of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Nagashima, a former BOJ executive director for international affairs. \par \par \uc2  Nagashima said he opposed the idea of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilised" by not draining excess yen funds from the interbank market. \par \par \uc2  He said that since the BOJ is coming under mounting pressure to ease credit to combat the yen's recent rise, it could respond by conducting \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the daily surplus, now about one trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  This which would help lower term money rates - those on instruments from one week to three months - and have an indirect effect on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, he said. \par \par \uc2  Nagashima said that since there are high expectations that finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven industrial powers may come up with concrete measures at a meeting on Saturday to correct the yen's rise, the lack of a concrete G7 agreement could risk pushing the dollar below 103 yen. \par \par \uc2  Japan's commitment to a looser fiscal and monetary policy could be a precondition for the United States to join Tokyo in selling yen for dollars, but such Japanese steps alone would not be enough to prompt the United States to act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market immediately, he said. \par \par \uc2  Washington, concerned that a concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might fail, thinks the timing of any such move is very important, he said. \par \par \uc2  European nations do not appear willing to help Japan fight the yen's rise by joining any such concerted action, Nagashima said. \par \par \uc2  But he argued: "European nations should have more interest in strengthening the euro against the yen to encourage Japanese investment in the euro and help stabilise European prices." ((Tatsuo Ito, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9l00c7w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
