\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-BOJ meets under pressure, some easing seen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1999\par 06:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 21 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan's Policy Board was meeting on Tuesday amid expectations it would ease monetary policy as a way to curb the strong yen, but doubts were growing over just how far the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would go. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect a reluctant BOJ, which has repeatedly rejected proposals to ease its already ultra-loose monetary policy, to fold under heavy pressure from policy-makers who fear a surging yen could undermine Japan's fragile economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa declined to comment on what he wanted the BOJ to do. But he said a senior ministry official attending the meeting would tell the nine-member board that the yen's recent rise has been "a bit rapid". \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa and other officials have made a show of arm-twisting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take a radical further loosening of policy, hinting that this was part of a deal to get U.S. authorities to join in yen-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Miyazawa confirmed overnight talk that his deputy Haruhiko Kuroda had not reached an agreement with his U.S. counterpart on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although he added that such discussions would talk place from here on. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa said he and U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers had agreed by phone last month that some steps might eventually be needed on the yen and he thought this time was coming. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese finance minister said he expects to discuss the strong yen with his Group of Seven counterparts in Washington on Saturday, although the G7's agenda was not formally set. \par \par \uc2  The yen has jumped 14 percent since early July and was trading around 107 to the dollar in mid-morning in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa also substantially confirmed that the United States was pressing the BOJ to ease policy and said Kuroda's visit to Washington last week largely set the stage for Tuesday's BOJ meeting. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial regulator, Hakuo Yanagisawa, said a BOJ easing would strengthen a basis for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. cooperation on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which had recovered to over 108 yen on Monday on expectations of a credit easing and joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , shed some two yen overnight as doubts grew about concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and whether the BOJ would go beyond token yen-weakening steps. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's repeated solo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this summer failed to restrain the yen from skyrocketing to 103.20 yen to the dollar last week, its highest since December 1995. \par \par \uc2  The yen's surge was sparked by an inflow of foreign money that flooded into Japan on hopes that an economic recovery was finally around the corner. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa summoned \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Masaru Hayami to a rare meeting last week, largely seen as a browbeating session, although Hayami emerged saying the bank's current policy of driving short-term rates to near zero was "more than enough". \par \par \uc2  Policy Board meetings have typically ended between 3:30 p.m. and 5 p.m. (0630-0800 GMT), but a few have come shortly after noon and some later in the evening. \par \par \uc2  With short-term interest rates already at around zero since last February and an official discount rate at a record low 0.5 percent for the past four years, the BOJ will have to select from a list of more arcane ways of increasing liquidity. \par \par \uc2  Among the more prominent: \par \par \uc2  * Leave yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilised" by not mopping up the excess yen from the interbank market. The BOJ has rejected this idea, which would aim to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value by prompting oversupply of it. \par \par \uc2  * Increase the daily fund surplus in the money market from the current amount of one trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  * Make outright purchases of short-term Japanese government bonds (JGBs) such as Treasury bills and financing bills. \par \par \uc2  * Increase the amount of fund injections via JGB repurchase \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  * Target term money market rates - those for periods of one week to three months. The bank now targets the overnight rate only. \par \par \uc2  * Target growth in some measure of money supply or inflation. \par \par \uc2  * Take steps such as a providing a special lending facility to ensure liquidity around the end of the year associated with the year 2000 computer bug. \par \par \uc2  * Cut the official discount rate, though this would be seen as largely symbolic in terms of monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9l00bzs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
