\par\par \b \uc2  Dow ends off 225 as U.S. trade deficit, yen rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Melville \par \uc2 708 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1999\par 23:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 21 (Reuters) - Stocks slid to one of their biggest losses of the year on Tuesday as Wall Street reeled from the one-two punch of a record U.S. trade deficit and Japan's refusal to slow the yen's speedy rise, which put more pressure on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average ended down 225.43 points, or 2.08 percent, at 10,598.47. The loss was the largest for the blue-chip index since a 235.23-point plunge on May 27. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq composite index tumbled 65.05 points, or 2.25 percent, at 2,821.10, a day after closing within just one point of its Sept. 10 record of 2,887.06. The Standard & Poor's index of 500 stocks fell 27.95 points, or 2.09 percent, to 1,307.58. \par \par \uc2  In the broader market, declining issues swamped advances 2,350 to 679 on moderate volume of 785 million shares on the New York Stock Exchange. \par \par \uc2  The market was dogged by news that the U.S. trade deficit widened to a record $25.18 billion in July, surpassing June's record of $24.60 billion. Economists polled by Reuters had expected the trade gap to shrink slightly to $23.8 billion. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, a move that triggered a drop in the dollar against the yen, pushing it down to 104.8 yen from 106.2 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also made only a token move to pump more money into its economy, bucking international pressures to lower interest rates and stem the yen's rise, which threatens to cut into its exports. \par \par \uc2  Last week, the dollar hit a three-and-a-half-year low of 103.1 against the yen, as Japan's economic recovery showed promise. \par \par \uc2  The 30-year U.S. Treasury bond was down 8/32, with the yield rising to 6.09 percent from 6.07 percent on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "We've been in a fade ever since we failed to rally on the good consumer price data last Wednesday," said Greg Nie, chief technical analyst at EVEREN Securities in Chicago. "That is being exacerbated by the very real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond concerns." \par \par \uc2  Contributing to the bearishness was a drop in semiconductor equipment stocks after an earthquake in Taiwan fanned concerns about business in the chip sector. \par \par \uc2  "It's the weakening in the dollar, the disaster in Taiwan, it's Apple Computer, it's a record trade deficit. It's a lot of things," said Peter Coolidge, senior equity trader at Brean Murray & Co. "It's just uncertainty and not knowing the situation that has the market jittery." \par \par \uc2  Apple Computer Corp. was one of the session's weakest issues, falling 9-13/16 to 69-1/4 on a huge volume of nearly 30 million shares after warning that its earnings would fall short of expectations. \par \par \uc2  Semiconductor equipment companies Applied Materials Corp. fell 2-1/2 to 81-5/16 and KLA-Tencor Corp. lost 4-1/2 to 66-15/16. \par \par \uc2  Among the 30 stocks that make up the Dow average, Hewlett-Packard Co. dropped 5-1/16 to 99-3/8, the largest point loss among Dow stocks. \par \par \uc2  Goodyear Tire & Rubber Co. tumbled 4-5/8 to 47-5/16 after J.P. Morgan Securities lowered its earnings outlook and rating on the shares, saying it expected Goodyear would have trouble meeting Wall Street estimates. \par \par \uc2  Seagram Co. Ltd. fell 5-1/8 to 46-3/8. The company issued a statement late in the session saying it was not aware of any material developments that could have accounted for the decline. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, gold jumped $6.00 to $261.80 an ounce on the New York market, buoyed by the dollar's weakness and solid demand for the precious metal at an auction by the Bank of England of a portion of its gold reserves. \par \par \uc2  Gold mining stocks rallied, with Newmont Mining Corp. up 1-15/16 to 21-13/16 and Barrick Gold up 1-1/8 at 19-13/16. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9l03bu7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
