\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - FOCUS-Bank of Japan bucks pressure, keeps policy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1999\par 13:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (CORRECTS day of week in lead, updates with comments from Hayami, Miyazawa) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 21 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan (BOJ) on Tuesday bucked government pressure to ease monetary policy - a victory for its independence but a setback for government efforts to stop a strong yen from undermining the economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The apparent defiance dashed market expectations of a further easing as part of a deal to gain U.S. help in pegging back the yen. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Policy Board voted by majority to continue guiding the key overnight interest rate virtually to zero. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ said it would provide more ample funds to the money market, the same phrase it has used since embarking on its zero interest rate policy in February. This leaves open the possibility that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could take more accommodative moves in its market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Japan preserved its independence by deciding to keep policy unchanged," said senior analyst Takehiro Sato at Sumitomo Bank. "It also strongly indicated that it would not conduct monetary policy aimed at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, which is logical." \par \par \uc2  But markets were taken by surprise, having already priced in some kind of monetary easing, with the dollar immediately plunging more than 1.5 percent against the yen to around 104.40. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, Japan is not very good at managing market expectations," said Gerard Lyons, global head of treasury research at Standard Chartered in London. "They tend to lead the market to water and then not allow the market to drink." \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and other officials have made a show of arm-twisting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into taking a radical further loosening of policy, hinting that this was part of a deal to get U.S. authorities to join in yen-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  These comments and Japanese media reports had fuelled expectations that the BOJ would take radical measures such as targeting longer-term interest rates or even leaving its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilised" by no longer mopping up the excess yen from the interbank market. \par \par \uc2  Market watchers looking for a "stealth" easing will be closely watching the BOJ's next money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 9:20 a.m. (0020 GMT) on Wednesday to see if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosts the daily surplus, currently about one trillion yen ($9.52 billion). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami also said he had instructed bank officials to consider using short-term government notes in market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that although it shares the government's concern that the recent yen rise could snuff out Japan's nascent recovery, it stressed that BOJ monetary policy is not and cannot be aimed at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  The bank noted in a statement that markets had been moving in recent days on speculation about its monetary policy, but asserting its independence, said: "There was no pre-arrangement of a certain course, or discussions with outsiders." \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa reacted stoically, declining to comment on the BOJ decision and saying it was understandable that an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have different views from the government on monetary policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The finance minister had hinted last week a deal was in the works, summoning Hayami to a rare and public meeting and dispatching his top international deputy to Washington to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of this Saturday's meeting of finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven industrial powers. \par \par \uc2  He confirmed earlier on Tuesday that his ministry had not sealed any deal with the U.S. Treasury on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but that this would be discussed "from here on". \par \par \uc2  Hayami told a news conference, however, that the yen rise is \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's problem and that concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be difficult to pull off. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which sank to nearly a four-year low around 103.20 yen last week, had rebounded as high as 108 yen on Monday on growing expectations of a deal including a credit easing and joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen's surge from the 120 level to the dollar in June has been sparked by a flood of foreign money investment in yen assets on hopes that an economic recovery is finally around the corner. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9l039jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
