\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Hopes for U.S-Japan dollar action fading. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1999\par 21:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Sept 21 (Reuters) - The prospect for joint U.S.-Japanese action to tame the rampant yen was fading fast on Tuesday after the Bank of Japan refused to bow to universal pressure to ease its already loose monetary policy further. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision came just four days ahead of a meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven top industrial nations. The yen's future - and that of the entire Japanese economy, the world's second-biggest - is expected to feature high on their agenda. \par \par \uc2  The yen has risen to its strongest level against the dollar in some 3-1/2 years, boosted by signs of strength in the long-depressed Japanese economy. But the gains have set off alarm bells in Tokyo, where officials worry over the strong yen's negative effect on the country's export performance. \par \par \uc2  A top Japanese finance official last week rushed to Washington to ask for help in capping the yen, seeking support for Tokyo's repeated selling of its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the open market. But they found a less-than-receptive audience, and apparently went home with no more than a promise for continued talks. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury, led by Lawrence Summers, has long called on Japan to use "all available means" to stimulate its economy - a veiled demand for monetary stimulus in the form of lower interest rates as a complement to multibillion-dollar spending programmes. \par \par \uc2  Whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  heeded such calls was seen as a crucial test for Japan's ability to convince Washington of the need for joint action on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Now that strategy appears to have run into a brick wall. \par \par \uc2  "The only leverage the Treasury has in terms of getting the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to loosen domestic policy is to hold off on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Adam Posen, a \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  expert at the Institute for International Economics. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's tight monetary policy is without question the single biggest risk to the G7 economies right now," he added. \par \par \uc2  Should the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's refusal to ease its monetary reins hurt Japan's frail recovery, U.S. officials fear the rest of Asia could feel knock-on effects just as it is starting to climb out of a deep recession. \par \par \uc2  But, short of trying to cajole Japanese authorities into action, Washington's policy options are severely limited - not least because of the Treasury's long-standing aversion to using market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and exchange rate levels as a tool to manage the economy. \par \par \uc2  "We should be a little bit worried when countries like Japan...regard the exchange rate as a counter-cyclical instrument," said Princeton University professor Peter Kenen. \par \par \uc2  That has long been Summers' own message. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. policy in this area continues to be what it has been - we believe that the maintenance of sustained, domestic demand-led growth in Japan is important for Japan and for the global economy," he told reporters on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  He declined to comment directly on Japan's policy decisions and instead simply repeated his long-standing mantra that a strong dollar was in the best interest of the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  With that, the ball is firmly in Japan's court, and the pressure on the Bank of Japan to act is likely to continue. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami, worried that any sign of caving in to outside pressure would compromise the independence that his institution won only two years ago, is in no mood to seek U.S. help on what he says is essentially a problem for his colleagues at the finance ministry. \par \par \uc2  Hayami told a news conference in Tokyo the yen's rise was \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's problem, adding that coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be "rather difficult" to pull off at this point. \par \par \uc2  The G7 finance ministers - representing Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan and the United States - are certain to ask Hayami to clarify his views when they meet in Washington on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Said Posen: "Everybody at this G7 meeting is going to be frustrated with the Bank of Japan, including the Japanese finance ministry." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9l038y7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
