\par\par \b \uc2  Yen may take aim at 100 as Japan holds policy line. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1999\par 20:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 21 (Reuters) - No news from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was good news for the yen on Tuesday, prompting a sharp jump against the dollar as dealers bet Washington will say little and do less to tame the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar shed more than two yen in hectic trade, and is now vulnerable to fall as low as 100 yen within days, traders predicted - far below the year's high of 125 yen. \par \par \uc2  The catalyst for fresh yen gains was the Bank of Japan's refusal overnight to buckle under growing calls for an even softer monetary policy aimed at stimulating domestic demand-led growth. \par \par \uc2  By holding its ground and voting to leave monetary policy unchanged, the bank may also have squandered a chance to secure international aid to tame yen strength at this weekend's Group of Seven most industrialised nations' meeting in Washington, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The yen soared on the news (of no BoJ action) as it would seem to reduce the likelihood of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the U.S. or even (the chance of seeing) a strongly worded communique," Mellon Bank chief strategist Marc Chandler said. \par \par \uc2  By midday U.S. trade, the yen's sharp gains had been curbed and the dollar was near 104.75 yen, about 1-1/2 yen below Monday's New York closing levels. But traders said there was little reason to stop buying yen here. \par \par \uc2  "There may be a few episodes of volatility this week, but after the Bank of Japan failed to act, it is unlikely the Group of Seven will help out and so by early next week the dollar may be trading below 100 yen," Banque Paribas chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader Dennis Heidt said. \par \par \uc2  In Washington, Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers urged Japan to maintain strong domestic-led economic growth and reiterated the administration's support for a strong dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. policy in this area continues to be what it has been - we believe that the maintenance of sustained, domestic demand-led growth in Japan is important for Japan and for the global economy, and we believe that a strong dollar is very much in the national interest of the United States," Summers told reporters after an appearance on Capitol Hill, \par \par \uc2  But the often repeated message failed to convince markets that Washington is unhappy about the dollar's decline against the yen, especially at a time of record trade deficits when cheaper U.S. goods can help even out the trade imbalance. \par \par \uc2  Dealers at Mellon Bank said the 103.20 yen level may be the last serious obstacle on the road to a test of 100 yen. \par \par \uc2  Technical analysts agreed, noting that charts suggest chances have improved that the dollar will soon break under 103.20 yen and then quickly slump to 100.30 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar could easily move toward 103.50 yen today," said ING Barings senior vice president John McCarthy. \par \par \uc2  As trading volumes dried up in the afternoon, dealers and analysts said the Bank of Japan was largely responsible for the yen's early morning ride and held the key to its future. \par \par \uc2  In the background, the positive yen scenario was underscored as several Wall Street investment houses recently raised their growth forecasts for Japan to between 1.5 and 2 percent for this year. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's news of America's record $25.18 billion trade gap in July, largely blamed on the dollar's strength at that time, compounded the yen's gains to some degree. Dealers again worried whether the United States will be able to attract enough investment to fund its growing current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  While the market fretted about the consequences of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest move and remained on alert for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen dips more, some analysts downplayed market hype. \par \par \uc2  Jesper Koll, chief Japan economist at Merrill Lynch, argued that current exchange rates were not unnerving either Tokyo or Washington - yet. \par \par \uc2  "Talk is cheap, but Japan itself has not spent very much money to harness the yen," Koll said. "While officials might prefer to have the yen trade near 110, it is unlikely that any alarm bells will go off before the yen hits levels between 95 and 85." \par \par \uc2  (( - NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9l038xp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
