\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market eyeballs BoJ's moves for new clues. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 1999\par 22:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 20 (Reuters) - Tuesday may become a red letter day for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if Japan agrees to ease its credit policy, seen as a key step toward clinching global help in taming the yen's strength, U.S. dealers said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Encouraged by Japan's improved economic outlook, the yen surged by 7 percent against the dollar in just a week after Tokyo released surprisingly strong growth data on September 9. The market stubbornly ignored two separate rounds of yen sales by the Bank of Japan yen aimed at stemming the rapid rise. \par \par \uc2  Last week, however, when Japan's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda hurried to the United States amid rumours about a possible deal between Tokyo and Washington, the yen reversed course to give back 4 percent of its earlier gain. \par \par \uc2  A closely scrutinised Bank of Japan policy board meeting on Tuesday will be crunch-time for Japan, said market participants, many of whom are betting the BOJ will be forced to ease its already loose monetary policy further. \par \par \uc2  "People are paying so much attention because the rhetoric suggests there may be a sort of quid pro quo," Mellon Bank vice president Grant Wilson said. The key issue, he added, remains whether Washington and Tokyo agree to any joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's short-term rates are already near zero and the official discount rate remains at a record low of 0.5 percent, dealers said the Bank of Japan may grudgingly agree to add new liquidity by no longer sterilising \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  For years, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has neutralised or sterilised its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by soaking up the yen sold through a money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  several days later. \par \par \uc2  Central banks generally prefer it this way because it allows them to keep total control over money supply growth. Now with Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  urging the step, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would let another agency meddle with its portfolio. \par \par \uc2  Washington is eager to see Japan end the sterilisation practice simply because it would be one more way Tokyo could stimulate domestic-demand-led growth, something the United States has been urging \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do for years. \par \par \uc2  "An unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is probably a minimum condition for the U.S. Treasury (to join any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," Citibank analyst T.J. Marta said, adding "all eyes are focused on less talk and more action" from Japan. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and BoJ have been at odds about this for months, but analysts said August's tamer money supply growth data may finally convince the bank that an additional easing, through unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is justified. \par \par \uc2  If the bank should balk, however, fearing a loss of face at a time of intense international pressure to move ahead with this, analysts said the bank could still add liquidity by increasing the daily fund surplus to more than its current one trillion yen through daily money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  There has also been more talk of another fiscal stimulus package which has been met with some signs of cooperation. \par \par \uc2  But dealers agreed there is no assurance of U.S. help, especially at a time the dollar remains relatively strong against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and U.S. officials repeatedly warned Japan to fix its own problems. \par \par \uc2  "The United States will not jump in automatically just if Japan says the right words," predicted Ricardo Gomes, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Republic National Bank. \par \par \uc2  In fact the market may not get hints of a deal until Finance Ministers and Central Bankers from the Group of Seven leading industrialised nations release a communique after their meeting in Washington on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Japan and the United States, joined by Germany, Italy, Canada, Britain and France, may single out the yen in their statement aiming to signal their unity and ability to act if markets move in a disorderly fashion, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But whether such a promise turns into \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains anyone's guess, market participants said, noting action in Tokyo is only the first piece of a puzzle that probably also includes calculations about how effective such a move may be. \par \par \uc2  "Central banks could probably get a lot more bang for their buck if they waited until the yen retreats from its overbought levels and starts to inch lower toward 109 where there is a key technical level. Then an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may actually have some lasting effect," Mellon's Wilson said. \par \par \uc2  ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9k00b43\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
