\par\par \b \uc2  U.S. stocks end up, bonds off, dollar falls vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jan Paschal \par \uc2 813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1999\par 01:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 20 (Reuters) - U.S. stock prices settled higher on Monday, with the Nasdaq index near a new high, while bonds slipped and the dollar fell against the Japanese yen before a key Bank of Japan policy meeting set for Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In the commodities markets, gold and oil fell. On Tuesday, the Bank of England will conduct its second auction of 25 metric tons of its gold reserves. On Wednesday, the ministers of the Organisation of Petroleum Exporting Countries will meet in Vienna. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average rose just 20.27 points, or 0.19 percent, to end at 10,823.90, on the slowest trading day of the year. Volume was thinned by observance Monday of the Jewish holiday Yom Kippur. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq composite index rose 16.53 points, or 0.58 percent, to 2,886.15 - just shy of its Sept. 10 record close of 2,887.06. The Standard & Poor's 500 stock index inched up a mere 0.11 of a point, or up 0.01 of a percent, at 1,335.53. \par \par \uc2  In the broader market, declining issues beat advances 1,749 to 1,160 as 564.9 million shares traded on the New York Stock Exchange. \par \par \uc2  On both the NYSE and Nasdaq markets, the gains could largely be linked to a few technology stocks. The Dow would have fallen if not for International Business Machines Corp. The computer maker surged 5-1/2 to 130-7/8, reversing last Friday's decline. \par \par \uc2  On Nasdaq. Sun Microsystems Corp. buoyed the index, rallying 5-7/8 to a new high 94-9/16 after announcing a stock split late Friday. \par \par \uc2  "We're living in a stock pickers' market," said Philip Orlando, chief investment officer at Value Line Asset Management. \par \par \uc2  General Electric Corp.,, one of the 30 Dow components, rose 2 to a 52-week high of 122. Merrill Lynch raised GE to a Focus 1 stock, or a top pick ranking, based on the conglomerate's strong management team and consistent earnings. \par \par \uc2  VoiceStream Wireless Corp., a provider of wireless communications systems, surged 5-7/8 to 61-15/16. VoiceStream agreed to buy Aerial Communications Inc., a digital cellular phone company majority owned by Telephone & Data Systems Inc., in a deal worth $1.8 billion. \par \par \uc2  Aerial climbed 6-5/8 to 26-5/8. And Telephone & Data, which cancelled a spin-off of Aerial as a result of the deal, added 4 to 79-11/16. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark 30-year U.S. Treasury bond slipped more than 1/4 point on Monday, pushing its yield up to 6.07 percent from 6.05 percent at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the Commerce Department releases the July U.S. trade deficit figures, which economists in a Reuters poll forecast would shrink slightly to $23.8 billion from a record $24.6 billion in June. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the dollar fell against Japan's yen as doubts arose over whether the Bank of Japan could enlist international help to tame its surging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has risen 14 percent versus the dollar since early July. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday in New York, the dollar slid to 106.25/35 yen, down nearly two yen from overnight highs at 108.16, and well below Friday's late U.S. trading level of 107.05/15. \par \par \uc2  Fears of possible joint U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had driven a brief yen fall in Asian and European trading hours. But by late in the New York trading day, those concerns had faded and the yen had shot higher. Looking ahead, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders focused on the Bank of Japan's monetary policy meeting on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The yen also rose against the euro, which slipped against the dollar Monday to stand at $1.0354/59 in late trade, down from $1.0409/16 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  COMEX December gold ended at $255.80 a troy ounce, down 10 cents. Many participants stayed on the sidelines ahead of Britain's gold-reserve sale on Tuesday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  On the New York Mercantile Exchange, crude oil for October delivery settled at $24.29 a barrel, down 43 cents. The October crude oil futures contract expires on Tuesday. The market is awaiting the outcome of the OPEC ministers' meeting, which begins Wednesday in Vienna, with the market watching to see whether oil-production cutbacks will be kept intact. \par \par \uc2  In London, the FTSE 100 index ended at 6,056.5, up 16.7 points, or up 0.28 percent. In Tokyo, the Nikkei average of 225 leading shares closed up 232.99 points, or 1.34 percent, at 17,575.26, on hopes that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will put the brakes on the yen's climb at its policy meeting Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9k00abf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
