\par\par \b \uc2  Bids seen limited at 10-yr JGB auction before BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Risa Maeda \par \uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 1999\par 12:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 20 (Reuters) - Uncertainty over the Bank of Japan's (BOJ) monetary policy is likely to keep investors from bidding actively in the government's auction of 10-year bonds on Tuesday, traders and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  An expected fall in the coupon rate for the October issue to 1.7 percent, the lowest for a monthly issued 10-year Japanese government bond (JGB) since June, is also likely to limit investor buying in the primary market, they said. \par \par \uc2  "We cannot get going so long as uncertainty remains over the BOJ's next move," said Yoshio Shukuwa, market economist at Sanwa Securities. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ holds its regular Policy Board meeting on Tuesday amid growing calls for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drop its opposition to a quantitative credit easing. \par \par \uc2  Markets are speculating that the Japanese authorities have gained the tentative support of the United States for a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise, but only on condition that Japan undertakes a substantive easing in monetary and fiscal policy. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government is widely expected to unveil the size of a supplementary budget for the current fiscal year in the coming weeks, but the BOJ has said it opposes a quantitative credit easing. \par \par \uc2  Although expectations of a credit easing sent yields lower on Monday, an actual easing on Tuesday could end up pushing long-term yields higher. \par \par \uc2  The current 10-year JGB yield level of around 1.7 percent could pave the way for a stable uptrend towards 1.8 percent, some dealers said, adding that if the BOJ takes further credit easing steps to boost the economy this would steepen the yield curve. \par \par \uc2  If the BOJ leaves its policy unchanged, the 10-year yield is likely to head lower, they said. \par \par \uc2  Shukuwa said if the BOJ keeps its monetary policy unchanged on Tuesday, the bond market would rally for the next couple of months as an even stronger yen will threaten the economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  But signs have emerged that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be willing to adopt some form of monetary easing. \par \par \uc2  "Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa and Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami met the other day and they are studying various steps, including quantitative (credit) easing," Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi said in a televised debate on Sunday evening. \par \par \uc2  "We need to create a situation where the world would cooperate," he added, a reference to Tokyo's campaign to persuade Washington to join in dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's short-term interest rates are already close to zero thanks to the BOJ's decision in February to guide the key overnight call rate as low as possible. \par \par \uc2  But critics and ruling politicians say if the BOJ refuses to take extra credit easing steps, it will be responsible for the economy stalling due to a higher yen. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's number-one priority is to implement the second supplementary budget without causing a further rise in long-term interest rates, say, to 2.5 percent," said a senior dealer at a major Japanese brokerage house. \par \par \uc2  In order to do so, the BOJ needs to announce some type of credit easing step as early as on Tuesday and put a lid on a rise in 10-year JGB yields at 1.8-1.9 percent, he said. \par \par \uc2  JGB yields have been rising for the past year, reflecting market expectations that Japan's worst recession since World War Two was over. \par \par \uc2  The yield of the key 10-year JGB fell to record low levels of below 0.7 percent around this time last year. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) has said it will offer 1.4 trillion yen worth of 10-year JGBs via an auction on Tuesday, unchanged from the last 10-year JGB auction. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the MOF is likely to set the coupon rate at 1.7 percent if the yield of the previously issued 215th September 10-year JGB stays at 1.65 percent or above early on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The yield of the 1.9 percent 215th issue fell to 1.650 percent in late trade on Monday, down from 1.705 percent on Friday, as traders bet on an imminent monetary easing. ((Risa Maeda, Tokyo Treasury Desk, +81-3-3432-8570 tokyo.news room@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9k009z2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
