\par\par \b \uc2  BOND MARKETS-Japan developments drive prices. \b0\par\par\uc2 590 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 1999\par 19:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 20 (Reuters) - Major government bond markets traded weaker on Monday after rallying early in the session on the prospect that the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  The prospect of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stemmed from talk the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will ease its already ultra-loose monetary policy further on Tuesday in a bid to quell the yen's alarming rise. \par \par \uc2  "We had started up quite buoyant because overnight there were stories that the Bank of Japan might do something on Tuesday. That boosted JGBs and that influence was felt in the European markets and the open of the U.S. Treasury market," said Aongus Buckley, an economist with GNI in London. \par \par \uc2  The possibility of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  easing and joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided a major boost for the dollar and euro against the yen. This in turn fed through to the underlying bond markets. \par \par \uc2  Many investors are expecting a decision on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be reached at the Group of Seven (G7) meeting on Saturday in Washington, D.C. \par \par \uc2  But early bond gains were rapidly reversed midway through the European session. Analysts said trade was driven by technical factors in thin volumes, rather than by any fundamental news. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's a small correction driven by technicals but there is nothing worrying so far," said Sanwa International trader Philippe Petit. \par \par \uc2  Buckley said a negative for European markets was a rumor that the German Ifo business climate index for August, due for release on Tuesday, would be stronger than expected. \par \par \uc2  Another concern for European investors is a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) meeting scheduled for Thursday. There is some market talk that the ECB may opt for a soft rate hike in the form of a variable rate. \par \par \uc2  The only euro zone data released on Monday was Italian consumer price inflation which rose 0.1 percent in August and 1.7 percent year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  U.S. data due out this week includes trade data for July on Tuesday, which is widely expected to influence the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. trade deficit worsened in June, rising to a record of $24.6 billion. In a poll by Reuters, economists called for the U.S. trade deficit in July to shrink slightly to $23.8 billion. The Commerce Department will release the July trade data at 0830 GMT/1230 EDT on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Jeffrey Hodgson, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 8663, fax +44 171 542 5285, jeffrey.jh.hodgson@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  This is how major world bond markets looked at 1656 GMT on \par \par \uc2  Monday \par \par \uc2      UNITED STATES - \par \par \uc2  Futures: December T-bonds 113-25/32, down   09/32 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab    December Eurodollars 94.070, down 0.020\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cash: Long bond at 100-25/32, down   07/32, yield 6.068 pct\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     EURO -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Futures: December Bunds on Eurex 105.63, down 0.23\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab    March Euribor 96.695, down 0.035\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cash:    10-year Bund 95.59, down 0.19 yield 5.081 pct\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRITAIN -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Futures: December Long gilts on LIFFE 106.86, down 0.32\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab  