\par\par \b \uc2  Miyazawa says hopes for uneventful day. \b0\par\par\uc2 218 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 1999\par 04:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 20 (Reuters) - Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa hoped wistfully on Monday for something the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have not provided of late - an uneventful day. \par \par \uc2  "It would be nice to have one day when nothing happened," Miyazawa told reporters ambushing him on his way into the ministry. \par \par \uc2  He did not otherwise respond to questions about the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where the dollar has bounced back to near 108 yen on hopes - that he himself helped stoke last week - of a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar had sunk last week to nearly four-year lows around 103 yen before Miyazawa said his top deputy was discussing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with his U.S. counterparts in Washington and the minister held a rare and highly public meeting with Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami. \par \par \uc2  The meeting gave the impression that the government was pressuring the BOJ to take radical measures to weaken the yen through further monetary easing. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi said the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were considering "quantitative" easing, which attempts to boost the money supply by pumping more yen into the market and thereby weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value. ((Ami Miyazaki, Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9k0091q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\pi