\par\par \b \uc2  With  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  deal brewing, focus on Bank of Japan. \b0\par\par\uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 1999\par 08:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 19 (Reuters) - With signs of a deal in the works to puncture the ballooning Japanese yen, traders will be focused on Tuesday, which could be the crucial day for a pact between Japan and the United States. \par \par \uc2  The yen soared last week to its highest level in 3-years, pushed up by foreign money flowing into Japan seeking a share in what looks like a long-awaited economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The worry is that the stronger yen, which hit the level of 103 to the dollar, could give such a body blow to the key export sector that the whole recovery would be endangered. \par \par \uc2  For Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, the problem was that he needed help from two opposing forces, the U.S. Treasury and the independent-minded Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Japanese authorities' recent single-handed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has failed to stem the rapid rise of the yen and the United States refused to go along, seeing the exercise as futile. \par \par \uc2  But under the deal worked up by Miyazawa last week, the United States would agree to joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  while the BOJ would accept some new measures to increase liquidity in the banking system. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ policy board meets on Tuesday, and could announce a change after that session. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa appeared confident on Friday at a news conference, saying he did not have any news just yet but hinting that things seemed to be coming together. \par \par \uc2  That was enough to help stabilise the dollar and send it up to 107 yen in New York trading on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The market is assuming that Japan will put something on the table to get backing from the U.S.," said Peter Stoneham, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Thomson Global Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  "That could take the form of additional stimulus measures or accepting that unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the way forward." \par \par \uc2  Sterilisation has become the buzzword in the recent debate. \par \par \uc2  After each round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ has entered the money market to soak up the extra yen it produced by buying dollars, in effect "sterilising" the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from money market policy. If it left the liquidity there, it would add more cash to the banking system and help degrade the value of the yen. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ argues that this is a risky notion that could sow the seeds of inflation later. It also argues that with the current policy of keeping short-term interest rates as low as you can get them, adding more liquidity is meaningless. \par \par \uc2  But supporters say it would provide a psychological boost to the idea that Japan is ready to act. \par \par \uc2  In the third act in a potential Tuesday drama, Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi could announce another round of government spending to keep the economy moving. That is the day that he will be, by all expectations, re-elected as head of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party, and therefore remain prime minister. \par \par \uc2  This would give him a good opportunity to announce more fiscal stimulus to help keep the economy on its surprisingly strong growth track this year. \par \par \uc2  BOJ action is by no means certain. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won new independence from the government under laws in April 1998 and BOJ governor Masaru Hayami has bristled at the idea of allowing the Finance Ministry to push it around. \par \par \uc2  One source close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  told Reuters that whatever the BOJ does, the word "unsterilised" would never be mentioned. \par \par \uc2  It appears more likely that the BOJ could increase its daily funds in the market, now at one trillion yen ($9.35 billion), or target a longer-term rate. \par \par \uc2  This would flatten the yield curve and offset pressure on rates because of higher government borrowing to pay for its spending packages. \par \par \uc2  It is also crucial for Miyazawa to get some tentative deal in place before he meets his counterparts of the G7 in Washington at the end of this week. ((Tokyo newsroom +813 3432-8018 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9j008zx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
