\par\par \b \uc2  Yen's Rally Raises Memories, Lessons Of 1995 \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michael Casey \par \uc2 1,118 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1999\par 17:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar's slide to a 44-month low against the yen this week sent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders back to their history books. \par \par \uc2  A nice recovery by the dollar Friday may not last if Tokyo doesn't back it up with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the buck. Believe it or not, the next big target for the downside is Y80.00, the dollar's lowest value ever against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and one that coincides with some critical events in the spring of 1995. \par \par \uc2  That's when the U.S. and other Group of Seven nations repeatedly bought dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that marked a turning point for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the start of the Treasury's "strong dollar" policy. \par \par \uc2  The question now is whether Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, who was Robert Rubin's deputy in 1995, will be as concerned this year by any further yen strengthening. \par \par \uc2  Comparing the two periods, Allen Sinai, chief economist with Primark Decision Economics, said direct action from the Treasury is unlikely this time around. \par \par \uc2  "The clever and well-timed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the U.S. (in 1995) occurred when the fundamentals were turning in the U.S. direction - namely that the U.S. economy was headed for a substantial pick-up and the Japanese economy was headed for a substantial period of weakness," Sinai said. \par \par \uc2  "In the current situation, the fundamentals are lining up against the dollar," Sinai continued. "The Japanese economy is headed for a cyclical recovery and ultimately higher returns on stocks and higher cash short-term interest rates, while the U.S. is headed for slower growth." \par \par \uc2  Maybe. But although the dollar, near Y107.00 Friday, is comfortably above Wednesday's low of Y103.20 and still a long way from 1995's levels, many believe the risks to Japan and to the global economy are as great now as they were four years ago. \par \par \uc2  Some evidence: Japan's all-important export sector is once again being squeezed by the strengthening yen, which makes Japanese products more expensive. At the same time, Asian economies are emerging from a crippling financial crisis and are unable to offer the same robust market for Japan's exports as they did in 1995. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Asia More Fragile Now\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A stronger yen also creates an incentive for those few Japanese\par companies that are still expanding to invest in new plants elsewhere in\par Asia - as they did in the early 1990's - rather than make much needed\par investments at home. To be sure, the excess capacity left by the Asian\par crisis makes large offshore capital movements unlikely, but it's also\par symbolic of how fragile the current situation is.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In 1995, the Japanese economy was still in a positive growth regime,"\par said Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist at NatWest Capital Markets. "All\par the sharp appreciation in the yen did was to make that growth\par considerably weaker. Now we have just barely seen two quarters of\par growth, and the risk is that this could be stopped dead in its tracks."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bhagavatula is hoping the Bank of Japan will "get religion" the same\par way it did in 1995 and embark on a more radical, stimulative monetary\par policy. Back then, the BOJ's decision to cut its discount rate by two\par percentage points complemented \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts because it made the\par yield on yen assets less attractive.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This time, many BOJ watchers are calling on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  not to\par "sterilize" its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In other words, allow\par the money supply to expand when it's next called upon to sell yen for\par dollars. Recent unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have had little\par lasting effect because the BOJ has mopped up the excess yen in the\par market. But if the overall supply of yen were to grow, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par price - it's exchange rate - would fall far more dramatically. So far,\par though, the BOJ has said it has no plans to change policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another complicating factors: there's no consensus support for more\par aggressive BOJ action. Indeed, views are widely spread on whether the\par dollar at Y107.00 represents an economic risk at all.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In 1995, by contrast, there was widespread agreement among economists,\par market participants and policy makers alike that the yen "was at an\par extreme low," said Jim McCormick, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at J.P. Morgan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A big difference between 1995 and 1999, McCormick said, is that the\par dollar's lows against the yen four years ago were "seen as dollar\par weakness." This year, it's a "yen strength" story, driven by a cyclical\par Japanese economic recovery, inflows of money into Japanese stocks and\par shifts into yen-based reserve holdings by Asian central banks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par McCormick and others also noted that although the dollar has fallen\par 18% against the yen, it's near its year highs against a basket of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a trade-weighted basis. That suggests there's isn't much\par 1995-style "dollar weakness" going on.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par               Monday Morning Quarterbacking\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yet in drawing contrasts between now and then, analysts can be caught\par out by tautology.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Just as the existence of an "asset bubble" in the stock market can\par only be proven after it bursts - as Federal Reserve Chairman Alan\par Greenspan recently pointed out - an "extreme" exchange rate, or a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market that's mispricing fundamentals, only becomes apparent\par once the market reverses course.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With the benefit of hindsight, it's clear the dollar was undervalued\par at Y80.00 in 1995, but before that reversal no one could know for sure.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Historical analysis can also steer attention toward the wrong\par variables. Indeed, the risk of yen overvaluation this year may not lie\par in its rate against the dollar, as it did in 1995, but against the euro.\par Using the Deutsche mark as a proxy for the single European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the\par yen is trading at its highest levels since World War II.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The question traders need ask, says Marc Chandler, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategist at Mellon Bank, is whether this euro level represent an\par "overshoot."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Referring to the events of 1995, Chandler says "one thing that comes\par through is that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets are prone to overshooting. Sometimes\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  do trade like Internet stocks."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The events of that year also showed that the G7 can find the political\par will to act effectively in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets when it sees fit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Should the yen strengthen to the point that its negative effect on\par Japan poses a risk to world growth, it's possible that the U.S., Europe\par and Japan will again feel compelled to act similarly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Michael Casey; 201-938-2009\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -michael.j.casey@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 17-09-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1557GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1142 4218 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9h06usx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
