\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Watch: May Buckle Under Pressure To Ease Before G-7 \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kenneth McCallum \par \uc2 905 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1999\par 12:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan could soon buckle under growing pressure to ease policy further to cap the yen's rapid rise, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  Some say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could decide to supplement its near-zero interest rate policy with new steps as early as Tuesday, when its policy board holds its next twice-monthly meeting. \par \par \uc2  The meeting will be the last before finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from the Group of Seven industrialized nations meet in Washington late next week. \par \par \uc2  Japanese reports have said the U.S. is moving toward conducting joint yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but only if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  follows up the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with additional monetary and fiscal steps. \par \par \uc2  With the dollar plunging to just above Y103.00 this week and the strong yen threatening to knock the wind out of the economic recovery, Japan's government appears to be stepping up pressure on the BOJ to comply with the U.S. request. \par \par \uc2  Many doubt that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can resist for much longer. \par \par \uc2  "The possibility has become strong" that the BOJ will loosen policy further, says Mitsuru Saito, chief economist at Sanwa Securities. "In the past, when the Bank of Japan's outer moats have been filled up and they were attacked on all sides, there haven't been many cases where they continued to say no." \par \par \uc2  Pressure is clearly building. In the past two days, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa has twice met with BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami in an apparent bid to put the screws on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief. \par \par \uc2  It's clear the two men discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy on both occasions, but both have been cagey about the specifics of the meetings. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par          Vague On Unsterilized \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One question is whether Miyazawa asked Hayami to stop absorbing excess\par yen from the money market after yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a process\par known as sterilization. In theory, leaving the funds unsterilized would\par undermine the value of the yen, making the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more effective.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thursday, Miyazawa said the topic wasn't raised in that day's meeting,\par but Friday morning Hayami was asked as he left another meeting if the\par subject had come up.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I don't know," Hayami muttered in response.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With short-term interest rates already effectively at zero,\par speculation is now building that the BOJ might be forced to seek looser\par monetary conditions in other ways.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nihon Keizai Shimbun (Nikkei) reported Friday evening that Japan\par and the U.S. are in the last stages of coming up with an agreement on\par joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that would be predicated on a move to ease by the\par BOJ.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As part of the plan, "the BOJ is expected to show its intention to\par loosen credit restraints further under its near-zero interest rate\par policy" at the Washington G-7 meeting, the Nikkei said without citing\par sources.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could ease conditions within the framework of its\par near-zero interest policy in several ways, analysts say.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One way would be to boost excess banking reserves by a couple of\par trillion yen from the Y1 trillion it usually leaves in the money market\par by conducting bond repurchase \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more frequently, Sanwa's Saito\par says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another way would be to leave the yen sold during \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unsterilized in the money market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Neither of these steps would make much of a difference for the money\par market, since the market is already flooded with funds that the BOJ\par pours in as part of its ultra-easy policy. But analysts say such steps\par might have the psychological effect of encouraging the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par to sell off the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        Change Could Undermine BOJ Independence\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In any case, there's some doubt that the BOJ will agree to conduct\par unsterilized \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , because doing so would go against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's stance that it conducts its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  only to adjust overall\par demand and supply of funds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ could also choose to buy more bonds to provide more liquidity\par or set targets for longer-term interest rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Past minutes from the BOJ's board meeting show the board has discussed\par most of these easing steps at one time or another, but members\par ultimately decided it would be too difficult or problematic to implement\par any of them.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although Hayami has said repeatedly the BOJ has done all it can with\par its easy policy, the government might argue its done its part to secure\par the U.S.'s agreement to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and now it's time for the BOJ to\par yield.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We should announce the contents and size of the second extra budget\par before the G-7" because it's important to get the U.S. to agree to\par coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Hakuo Yanagisawa, minister for financial\par reconstruction, said Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yanagisawa met with U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan in\par Washington earlier this month.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One factor further complicating the policy debate: The BOJ can't\par afford to look like it's being bullied so soon after key laws took\par effect in April, 1998, to bolster its independence.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If the Bank of Japan changes the way it conducts its policy, that\par would bring up the thorny issue of the Bank of Japan's independence,"\par says Ryutaro Kono, senior economist at Dai-Ichi Life Research Institute.\par    -By Kenneth McCallum; 813-5255-2938; kmccallum@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 17-09-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1003GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1042 4131 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv9h06uh7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
