\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  anxiety buoys dollar in U.S. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lida Poletz \par \uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1999\par 16:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The dollar was buffeted against the yen in thin, volatile U.S. trade on Friday but clung to most of its steep overnight gains as speculation about coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action spooked the market. \par \par \uc2  The yen plunged 2.0 percent against the dollar and 2.5 percent versus the euro overnight, following comments from Japanese officials suggesting that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other central banks was becoming more likely. \par \par \uc2  By early U.S. trade the dollar was oscillating between 106.30 and 106.80, off its overnight peak of 107.33 yen, its highest level since Monday. \par \par \uc2  At 0830 EDT the dollar stood at 106.30/40 yen, still up sharply from 105.05/15 at the previous U.S. close. The euro stood at 110.80/90 yen, off a full yen from overnight peaks. \par \par \uc2  More choppy trade was expected, traders said, with further dollar gains probably limited, barring \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  "The amounts of volume going through are not huge. It's mostly speculators taking profits," said Mitsuaki Kataoka, vice president at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  "The overnight move from 105 to 107 - maybe it is too much for one day's move, and with it being the final day of the week I don't think the market will be so liquid," he said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. traders were mostly sceptical that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would launch a fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this session, with or without international help. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think there's going to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  today or over the weekend," said John Hazelton, trader at PNC Bank. "They're accomplishing what they want with talk of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said in Tokyo he expected to discuss the yen in Washington next week when he attends a meeting of the Group of Seven industrialised nations. He added he no longer thought battling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength was Japan's problem alone. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa also said it was "possible" that his deputy Haruhiko Kuroda was discussing joint US-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with U.S. Treasury officials during a visit to Washington that started on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The yen has gained over 15 percent against the dollar from the year lows in May amid signs the economy was starting to recover, and government officials fear a too-rapid rise will hurt the economy. \par \par \uc2  Speculation revolved around the possibility the United States may join Japan in controlling the yen, and that the Bank of Japan may be prepared to take credit-easing action such as unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past four months have been sterilised, with excess liquidity drained from the market, and have had little lasting impact. \par \par \uc2  With all eyes on the yen, trade between the dollar and the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sidelined. The euro traded at $1.0388/93 at 0830 EDT, barely changed from $1.0370/75 in late Thursday U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The British pound slid modestly against the dollar to $1.6178/88, from $1.6225/30. But it briefly surged to a record high aganst the euro of 63.76 pence after a key policymaker suggested the Bank of England may be prepared to acept a higher long-term exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell against the Canadian dollar to C$1.4702/12, from C$1.4753/63, but the Australian dollar advanced to $0.6490/95, from $0.6465/70. \par \par \uc2  ((N.A. Treasury Desk, +1 212 859 1868)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9h00fpj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
