\par\par \b \uc2  BOND MARKETS - Prices firm as yen retreats. \b0\par\par\uc2 577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1999\par 18:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Global bond markets ended the week on a stronger note as the yen fell from recent highs and as the impact of U.S. investors closing their books early due to poor weather fed through. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the main focus of the market this week as it continued its sharp rise against the dollar and the euro even after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice to curb the rise. \par \par \uc2  But both the dollar and the euro rebounded against the yen on Friday as speculation increased that there might be bilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though some Japanese officials later played it down. The dollar's gains also boosted Treasuries. \par \par \uc2  The early close in the U.S. markets on Thursday due to Hurrican Floyd continued to have a positive impact. \par \par \uc2  "The early close of U.S. markets yesterday to beat the bad weather has forced a round of position squaring and profit-taking that has had mutual spin-off effects both for bonds and the dollar," said David Brown, economist at Bear Stearns International. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said for the first time on Friday that he expects to discuss the yen in Washington next week at the meeting of the Group of Seven leading industrial nations. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 106.89 at 1531 GMT, up from 105.05/15 yen in New York late on Thursday. The U.S. long bond yield fell by 2.5 basis points to 6.054 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Euro debt has been boosted by a dollar-induced rally in Treasuries," said Nick Stamenkovic, senior bond strategist at IDEAglobal.com. \par \par \uc2  "There has been some increased speculation that there may be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which was supportive for the dollar, and prices racked up in Treasuries on the back of it," he said. \par \par \uc2  European government bonds were also given a boost following comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) President Wim Duisenberg on Thursday that the outlook for European inflation looked positive. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with an Irish newspaper, Duisenberg gave indications that the ECB intends to keep interest rates stable until the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  Friday's data calendar was light in both Europe and the U.S. and economic indicators failed to move the markets. \par \par \uc2  Euro debt markets will have a busy schedule next week, with the ECB meeting on Thursday and a lot of economic data releases. The most closely watched economic release will be the August German Ifo business climate index out on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  British government bonds held sizeable gains on Friday, with the market underpinned by sterling's strength. There was little in the domestic diary to provide direction. \par \par \uc2  This is how major world bond markets looked at 1649 GMT \par \par \uc2  on Friday \par \par \uc2      UNITED STATES - \par \par \uc2  Futures: December T-bonds 114-03/32, up   18/32 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab    December Eurodollars 94.085, up 0.015\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cash: Long bond at 100-31/32, up   11/32, yield 6.054 pct\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     EURO -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Futures: December Bunds on Eurex 105.91, up 0.31\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab    March Euribor 96.735, up 0.025\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cash:    10-year Bund 95.81, up 0.30 yield 5.051 pct\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BRITAIN -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Futures: December Long gilts on LIFFE 107.26, up 0.74\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab  