\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP-Japan Miyazawa presses BOJ, US to help on yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 896 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1999\par 11:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa stepped up his offensive to enlist help from the Bank of Japan and U.S. authorities to tame the rampant yen on Friday, appearing confident that a deal was in the works. \par \par \uc2  Although coy on details, Miyazawa said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent surge had caused him to change his view, and he no longer thought that battling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was Japan's problem alone. \par \par \uc2  He also said for the first time that he expects to discuss the yen in Washington next week, when he will attend a meeting of the Group of Seven industrial nations. \par \par \uc2  The yen, which retreated over two yen on Friday amid talk of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had strengthened some 13 percent since Japanese data in June began showing a revival in the world's second-largest economy as money has poured into Japanese assets. \par \par \uc2  Late on Friday in Tokyo it was around 106.70 to the dollar. The deal Miyazawa is pushing would likely have Washington join Japan in selling yen for dollars and the Bank of Japan (BOJ) would embark on further monetary easing to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Given that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already pushed short-term interest rates to around zero, this would involve more arcane ways of force-feeding the banking system, such as leaving the yen it sells in the market to circulate rather than soaking them up through bond sales, a process dubbed "sterilisation". \par \par \uc2  Financial daily Nihon Keizai Shimbun said just such a deal was being readied, reporting that Washington would join \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in large yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that the BOJ would tell the G7 it would embark on a quantitative easing. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi joined the fray, saying Japan must consider every step to deal with the high yen, "including cooperation with other countries". He also said he was "aware of influential opinions" favouring a further BOJ easing. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa has sent a top deputy to see U.S. officials and has huddled twice in as many days with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief. Asked whether his deputy Haruhiko Kuroda had discussed joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday in Washington, Miyazawa told a news conference: "No one is saying so, but I think it is possible they are discussing the issue." \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa also let drop that Hayami had telephoned William McDonough, president of the New York Federal Reserve, which conducts the Fed's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On further monetary policy steps by the BOJ, he said with a smile: "It's not appropriate for me to answer that right now." \par \par \uc2  Economists and market players say Tokyo's recent go-it-alone \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market cannot succeed unless joined with yet more pumping of money into the economy or joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Market speculation was rising that some form of package might be compiled, including U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , BOJ easing and perhaps even extra Japanese government spending ahead of the September 25 meeting of G7 finance ministers and central bankers. \par \par \uc2  The government says that in general a rise in the yen is a sign of a welcome "buy Japan" mentality but frets that the recent surge could smother the nascent recovery that engendered it. \par \par \uc2  The government on Friday maintained for a third month its assessment that the economy was "improving somewhat", but an Economic Planning Agency official singled out the yen's recent rise as a risk for the nascent recovery. \par \par \uc2  A stronger yen erodes the value of Japanese firms' overseas earnings and puts deflationary pressure on the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa met briefly with Hayami on Friday morning after summoning the central banker the day before to a rare and high-profile meeting that left the strong impression of arm-twisting for further monetary easing. \par \par \uc2  After Thursday's talk, both said they agreed on the need to respond to the yen's rise but Hayami showed no indication of budging on monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  He insisted to reporters the BOJ was doing "more than enough" by pumping enough funds into the Tokyo money market to drive short-term interest rates virtually to zero. \par \par \uc2  But markets wonder if the BOJ, which has repeatedly rejected proposals to target a boost in money supply in a further, unorthodox easing, can hold up in the face of rising pressure. \par \par \uc2  Some politicians, economists and Miyazawa's just-retired international deputy Eisuke Sakakibara have called for the BOJ to get on board with further easing to augment the yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  it conducts under Finance Ministry instructions. \par \par \uc2  But Hayami reiterated on Thursday that the BOJ would continue to sterilise its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Other moves being urged on the BOJ were to increase outright buying of Japanese government bonds in the secondary market or underwrite debt directly from the government - steps it has rejected in the past. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury has given no public indication of interest in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost the dollar, and is widely thought to oppose an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it could not win. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say, however, that an accumulation of large net long yen positions by speculators on the Chicago Mercantile Exchange - the biggest in five years or more - means the yen could retreat rapidly if the tide began to turn in the dollar's favour. ((Tokyo newsroom +81-3 3432-8022 email: tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9h00f2l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
