\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen retreats on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deepthi Wickremasinghe \par \uc2 1,164 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1999\par 12:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The yen retreated around two percent against the dollar and 2.5 percent against the euro by midsession on Friday amid light trade and market nervousness about bilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Prime Minister Keizon Obuchi said earlier in the European session Japan must consider every possible step to counter the yen's rise, including cooperation with other countries. He was echoing comments of several other Japanese officials overnight. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said overnight it was possible Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda and U.S. Treasury official Timothy Geithner had discussed joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they met in Washington on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "People are worried ahead of the weekend in case some agreement is reached and we see concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But if we get in on Monday and nothing has happened then the risk must be that dollar/yen goes back down," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank. \par \par \uc2  "The threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as we have seen before, can only hold it up for so long." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 107.26/31 yen by 1009 GMT off a day's low of $104.80 and compared with 105.05/10 late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was tracking the dollar's path against the yen and was at 111.63/74 yen, against a low of 108.91 set earlier and 109.25/35 in Thursday's late European trade. \par \par \uc2  TREND INTACT \par \par \uc2  James McKay, global market strategist at National Australia Bank, said the firmer tone in dollar/yen on Friday had been helped by investors' need to cover short positions. \par \par \uc2  "The long term trend in dollar/yen hasn't turned," he said. "We should head down towards 100 and possibly through that level, but clearly the market needs to get on a more even bias." \par \par \uc2  In the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ferridge at Rabobank said dollar/yen could get back below the 105/104 area but did not see a test of the 100 yen level ahead of a meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors on September 25. \par \par \uc2  However, some analysts said it was likely to take longer for the yen to resume its upward path. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese authorities have committed themselves to do something with their strong rhetoric today, either credit easing or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a trader at a U.S. bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "It might not happen at these levels, but if nothing happens within the next two weeks the market will test the downside again for dollar/yen." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the euro's rise against the yen was largely yen driven with little change in the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's levels against the dollar overnight. \par \par \uc2  The euro was last at $1.0401/06 compared with $1.0407/09 late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  McKay at National Australia Bank said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's September monthly report released late in Europe on Thursday offered little new news to the market. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB does seem a little bit concerned about money supply and credit growth in the euroland economy and I suspect they will raise rates, but we will probably have to wait a couple of months for that to occur," he said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro was edging up from life low against sterling set earlier in Europe at 63.76 pence. Euro/sterling was last at 64.26/31 pence versus 64.09/11 in late European trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The pound was initially buoyed by comments from Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee member Sushil Wadhwani, who said late on Thursday that sterling might stay higher than assumed in the Bank's August inflation report. \par \par \uc2  Sterling later declined amid talk of corporate activity and speculation, dismissed by analysts and traders, that the Bank of England may have sold sterling. \par \par \uc2  "If they were in it is more likely to have been money market management rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because to get \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to work you need to make as much noise about it as possible," said National Australia Bank's McKay. ((London capital markets, +44 171 542 7792, fax +44 171 542 5285, deepthi.wickremasinghe@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  James McKay, global market strategist at National Australia Bank, said the firmer tone in dollar/yen on Friday had been helped by investors' need to cover short positions. \par \par \uc2  "The long term trend in dollar/yen hasn't turned," he said. "We should head down towards 100 and possibly through that level, but clearly the market needs to get on a more even bias." \par \par \uc2  In the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ferridge at Rabobank said dollar/yen could get back below the 105/104 area but did not see a test of the 100 yen level ahead of a meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors on September 25. \par \par \uc2  However, some analysts said it was likely to take longer for the yen to resume its upward path. \par \par \uc2  